window.QUIZ_DATA={"1":{"title":"Primary Production of Milk","q":[["Approximately what proportion of cow's milk is water?","87%","50%","65%","99%","Cow's milk consists of around 87% water and 13% dry substance."],["Which hormone is associated with the milk let-down reflex?","Oxytocin","Insulin","Adrenaline","Melatonin","Oxytocin causes contraction of cells around the alveoli and supports milk let-down."],["How long does the milk let-down effect normally last?","5–8 minutes","1–2 minutes","15–20 minutes","30 minutes","The handbook gives a normal let-down duration of about 5–8 minutes."],["What operating principle is used by a conventional milking machine?","Vacuum","Gravity only","Centrifugal force","Compressed steam","Conventional milking machines use vacuum at the teat cups."],["Fresh milk leaves the udder at approximately what temperature?","37 °C","4 °C","15 °C","72 °C","Fresh milk leaves the udder at about body temperature, around 37 °C."],["Why should milk be cooled rapidly after milking?","To restrict bacterial growth","To increase lactose content","To raise the milk pH","To enlarge fat globules","Rapid cooling limits bacterial growth in freshly collected milk."],["What temperature is commonly targeted for farm milk cooling?","About 4 °C","About 20 °C","About 40 °C","About 72 °C","The handbook repeatedly uses about 4 °C as the target for chilled raw milk."],["Which animal is described as having no cisternal milk fraction between milkings?","Buffalo","Cow","Goat","Sheep","Buffalo milk is held in the glandular part of the udder until milk ejection."],["Why is the massage phase used in machine milking?","To relieve the teat and prevent blood/fluid congestion","To heat the milk","To sterilize the liner","To increase fat percentage","Alternating suction and massage prevents continuous vacuum from causing congestion and discomfort."],["For very large milk volumes, what can supplement bulk-tank cooling?","An in-line heat exchanger or plate cooler","A larger teat cup","A higher pulsation frequency","A milk separator","Large farms may cool milk in-line before it enters the storage tank."],["A farm’s bulk tank already contains milk at 4 °C. Why is an in-line plate cooler useful before newly milked milk enters the tank?","It prevents warm milk from reheating the already chilled milk","It increases milk fat content before storage","It replaces the need for tank agitation","It raises the milk temperature gradually to improve stability","Pre-cooling reduces the thermal load on the tank and avoids mixing warm milk directly with chilled milk."],["A milking machine applies vacuum continuously without an effective massage phase. What problem is most directly expected?","Congestion of blood and tissue fluid in the teat","Rapid freezing of milk in the liner","Immediate loss of lactose from the milk","A higher pH in the udder","The massage phase relieves the teat from continuous vacuum and helps prevent congestion."],["Why must milking equipment be designed to minimize air intake?","Aeration can impair milk quality and later processing","Air immediately destroys all milk proteins","Air raises the freezing point enough to reject the milk","Air eliminates the need for cooling","Gentle, low-aeration handling is emphasized from milking onward because entrained air creates later processing problems."],["A farm produces enough milk that tank cooling cannot bring the whole volume to 4 °C within two hours. Which design change best follows the handbook?","Add an external plate cooler before the storage tank","Increase the storage temperature to 8 °C","Reduce cleaning frequency to save cooling capacity","Use a smaller tank with no agitation","Large farms often use plate coolers ahead of the tank when tank refrigeration alone is insufficient."],["Why is rapid cooling more important when milk collection occurs every second or third day?","The milk must remain microbiologically stable for a longer pre-processing period","Longer collection intervals reduce bacterial risk automatically","Cooling becomes less important because milk settles","Milk fat becomes sterile after 24 hours","Longer storage before processing increases the need to suppress microbial growth."],["During milk let-down, why is timing of the milking process important?","The oxytocin-driven let-down response is temporary","The milk becomes chemically unstable after exactly one minute","The fat content drops to zero after eight minutes","Vacuum can only be generated for a short period","The handbook notes that the let-down effect typically lasts only about 5–8 minutes."],["Which combination best describes a well-designed farm milk room?","Refrigerated storage plus facilities for cleaning and disinfection","Warm storage plus open-air ventilation only","No drainage, to avoid contamination from outside","Only a bulk tank, with no cleaning facilities required","Preserving milk quality requires both cooling and hygienic cleaning of utensils, pipelines and tanks."],["If fresh milk leaves the udder at about 37 °C and is not cooled promptly, what is the main process risk?","Microorganisms can multiply rapidly before the dairy receives the milk","Casein immediately precipitates at neutral pH","Milk fat becomes completely solid","The freezing point falls far below normal","Warm raw milk provides favorable conditions for microbial growth."],["Why is gentle agitation used in a farm cooling tank instead of vigorous mixing?","To equalize temperature without excessive mechanical treatment or aeration","To create foam for better cooling","To increase oxidation for flavor development","To deliberately break fat globule membranes","The handbook consistently emphasizes gentle handling and avoiding unnecessary aeration."],["Which farm-side decision most directly supports both product quality and later process reliability?","Fast cooling combined with hygienic milking and low-aeration handling","Increasing raw milk temperature before transport","Allowing repeated warming and cooling cycles","Delaying cleaning until the tank is visibly dirty","Milk quality preservation begins at the farm through hygiene, cooling and gentle handling."]]},"2":{"title":"The Chemistry of Milk","q":[["Which is the principal constituent of cow's milk?","Water","Fat","Protein","Lactose","Cow's milk is about 87% water."],["What is the normal pH range of fresh milk stated in the handbook?","6.6–6.8","4.0–4.6","7.5–8.0","2.0–3.0","Normal milk has a pH of about 6.6–6.8, averaging around 6.7."],["About what proportion of milk protein is casein?","Four-fifths","One-tenth","One-half","All of it","Casein accounts for roughly four-fifths of the protein in milk."],["At approximately what pH is casein precipitated by acid in practice?","4.6","6.7","7.0","9.0","The practical isoelectric precipitation point for casein is around pH 4.6."],["What are triglycerides built from?","Glycerol and fatty acids","Lactose and minerals","Casein and whey","Water and salts","Triglycerides consist of glycerol combined with fatty acids."],["What is lactose?","The principal carbohydrate in milk","The main milk fat","A mineral salt","A milk enzyme","Lactose is the characteristic carbohydrate of milk."],["Which protein fraction remains in the serum after casein coagulation?","Whey proteins","Casein micelles","Milk fat","Lactose crystals","Whey proteins remain in the liquid serum when casein is coagulated."],["What is an amino acid in relation to milk protein?","A building block of protein","A type of milk fat globule","A mineral crystal","A sugar molecule","Proteins are built from chains of amino acids."],["Milk fat crystallization is described as what kind of reaction?","Exothermic","Endothermic only","Photochemical","Electrolytic","The handbook notes that milk-fat crystallization releases heat."],["Why can milk proteins act as ampholytes?","They can accept or release hydrogen ions depending on pH","They contain no charged groups","They are insoluble at all pH values","They are made only of fat","Amino-acid groups give proteins acid-base behaviour that depends on pH."],["A milk sample is acidified toward pH 4.6. What structural change is expected in the casein system?","Casein micelles lose net charge and aggregate near their isoelectric point","Whey proteins become more negatively charged indefinitely","Lactose crystallizes because pH alone reaches freezing conditions","Fat globules dissolve into the serum phase","Near the isoelectric point, casein micelles have little net charge and can precipitate."],["Why can calcium and phosphate behavior change during heating or storage of milk?","Milk salts exist in equilibria between soluble and colloidal forms","All minerals are permanently fixed inside fat globules","Milk salts are chemically inert under all process conditions","Only lactose controls mineral distribution","The handbook describes milk salts as dynamic equilibria that can shift with process conditions."],["A process engineer needs to separate water and small dissolved solutes from larger milk components using a membrane. Which principle is most relevant?","Selective transport driven by concentration or pressure differences","Crystallization of all milk proteins","Mechanical churning of fat globules","Pasteurization by direct steam only","The chemistry chapter introduces diffusion, osmosis and reverse osmosis as separation principles."],["Why does the iodine value provide information about the physical character of milk fat?","It reflects the degree of unsaturation and therefore relates to fat softness","It directly measures protein concentration","It gives the pH of the serum","It measures the number of fat globules per millilitre","More unsaturation changes melting behavior, and iodine value reflects double-bond content."],["Why is beta-lactoglobulin important during heating of milk?","It is a major whey protein that denatures above about 60 °C and can participate in heat-induced changes","It is the main carbohydrate in milk","It is the mineral responsible for freezing-point depression","It is the only protein in casein micelles","The handbook notes beta-lactoglobulin denaturation and its contribution to cooked flavor chemistry."],["A milk protein has equal positive and negative charge at a certain pH. What term describes this condition?","Isoelectric point","Osmotic pressure","Iodine value","Freezing point","At the isoelectric point the protein has no net electrical charge."],["Why does milk behave simultaneously as an emulsion, colloid and true solution?","Different constituents exist at different size scales and physical states","All constituents are dissolved molecularly","All constituents are suspended as large particles","Milk is only an oil-in-water emulsion with no dissolved phase","Fat, casein and dissolved lactose/minerals occupy different dispersed or dissolved states."],["Why is SNF useful in milk composition calculations?","It represents total solids excluding fat","It represents only casein and whey proteins","It is another name for milk fat","It is the water fraction of milk","Solids-not-fat equals total solids minus fat."],["If milk pH falls well below its normal 6.6–6.8 range, what is a likely implication for protein stability?","Casein moves closer to its isoelectric region and becomes more prone to coagulation","Fat becomes completely soluble in water","Lactose disappears from solution","All whey proteins become casein","Acidification reduces casein charge and promotes aggregation."],["Why can heat treatment alter flavor as well as protein functionality?","Heat can denature whey proteins and generate sulfur-containing compounds","Heat removes every mineral ion from milk","Heat converts all lactose directly to fat","Heat makes pH exactly neutral","The handbook links whey-protein denaturation and sulfur compounds to cooked flavor development."]]},"3":{"title":"Rheology","q":[["According to Chapter 3, what is rheology?","The science of deformation and flow of matter","The science of heat transfer through food products","The study of microorganisms in liquid foods","The measurement of milk composition","Rheology is defined as the science of deformation and flow of matter."],["Which two properties are used in the handbook to describe consistency rheologically?","Viscosity and elasticity","Density and temperature","Pressure and flow rate","Fat content and protein content","The handbook describes consistency in terms of viscosity and elasticity."],["At a specified temperature, how does the viscosity of a Newtonian fluid respond to a change in shear rate?","It remains constant","It always increases","It always decreases","It becomes zero above a critical shear rate","For a Newtonian fluid, viscosity is independent of applied shear rate at a given temperature."],["Which dairy products can, for practical purposes, be treated as Newtonian fluids?","Whole milk and skim milk","Yoghurt and mayonnaise","Quark and tomato paste","Cream and yoghurt","Whole milk and skim milk may practically be characterized as Newtonian."],["A yoghurt becomes easier to pump as shear rate increases. What type of behaviour is this?","Shear-thinning","Shear-thickening","Purely elastic","Newtonian","Shear-thinning means apparent viscosity decreases as shear rate increases."],["Which is a typical shear-thickening example in the chapter?","Concentrated starch suspension","Whole milk","Skim milk","Water","Concentrated starch suspensions are used as examples of shear-thickening systems."],["What happens to viscosity in a shear-thickening fluid when shear rate increases?","It increases","It decreases","It remains constant","It becomes zero","A shear-thickening fluid becomes more viscous as shear rate increases."],["What distinguishes a plastic fluid from a fluid that simply has a high viscosity?","It has a yield stress that must be exceeded before significant flow begins","It must be heated before it flows","Its viscosity is always constant","It cannot store deformation energy","Plastic fluids exhibit yield stress; the stress must be exceeded before significant flow begins."],["A yoghurt's viscosity continues to decrease with time while held at a constant shear rate. What does this indicate?","Thixotropic behaviour","Newtonian behaviour","Shear-thickening behaviour only","Pure elastic behaviour","Thixotropic fluids decrease in viscosity with time at a constant shear rate."],["Why should viscosity data for process design be measured at representative shear rates and temperatures?","Because product viscosity and non-Newtonian behaviour depend on actual process conditions","Because every dairy product becomes Newtonian at high shear","Because viscosity depends only on product colour","Because temperature has essentially no effect on viscosity","Measurements should represent the shear rates and temperatures of the actual process."],["A product follows the Herschel-Bulkley model with n below 1 and a non-zero yield stress. How should it be described?","A yield-stress, shear-thinning fluid","A Newtonian fluid with no yield stress","A shear-thickening fluid with zero yield stress","A purely elastic solid","In the generalized power-law model, n<1 indicates shear thinning and a non-zero yield stress indicates plastic behavior."],["Why is a single viscosity value often insufficient for a non-Newtonian dairy product?","Apparent viscosity depends on shear rate and temperature","Viscosity is independent of process conditions","Only density matters for non-Newtonian products","Viscosity can only be measured at atmospheric pressure","Non-Newtonian viscosity must be linked to measurement conditions."],["A yoghurt shows a large hysteresis loop in a controlled shear test. What does this primarily indicate?","Time-dependent structural breakdown and recovery","Purely Newtonian behavior","No sensitivity to shear history","A constant viscosity independent of time","Loop tests are used to characterize thixotropic time dependence."],["Why is a narrow, well-defined measuring gap important when testing a non-Newtonian product?","It allows the shear rate to be defined accurately","It guarantees the product becomes Newtonian","It eliminates temperature effects","It makes yield stress impossible","Quantitative rheology requires known shear conditions."],["A pump speed is increased and the apparent viscosity of a yoghurt falls. Which process consequence is most likely?","Pressure drop may not rise in direct proportion to flow as it would for a Newtonian fluid","The yoghurt must have become shear-thickening","The product has lost all elasticity permanently","The density must have doubled","Shear-thinning changes the relationship between flow, viscosity and pressure loss."],["For Herschel-Bulkley behavior, what does n=1 imply if yield stress is zero?","Newtonian behavior","Shear-thickening behavior","Thixotropy","Pure elasticity","With n=1 and no yield stress, the model reduces to Newtonian flow."],["Why might a tubular viscometer be preferred for a product containing particles?","It can accommodate particulate products better than narrow-gap rotational geometries","It eliminates all pressure drop","It measures only elasticity","It requires no known dimensions","The handbook notes tubular viscometers are suitable for particulate products, though bulky and product-demanding."],["A rheometer uses small oscillatory deformation on yoghurt. What is the advantage?","Viscoelastic structure can be studied without fully destroying it","It forces the product to become Newtonian","It measures only density","It removes the need for temperature control","Oscatory testing can probe structure with limited damage."],["Why can a three-degree temperature change materially affect viscosity data?","The handbook notes even a few degrees can change viscosity by roughly ten percent","Temperature has no effect on viscosity","Only freezing-point changes matter","Viscosity changes only above 100 °C","Temperature control is essential for meaningful rheological data."],["What is the main danger of using an instrument that gives an undefined shear rate for process calculations?","The reading may be empirical and not transferable to actual process conditions","The instrument will always read zero","The sample will necessarily coagulate","The result automatically becomes a yield stress","Without a defined shear rate, quantitative comparison and process design are unreliable."]]},"4":{"title":"Microbiology","q":[["What is microbiology?","The study of microscopic living organisms","The study of milk flow only","The study of heat exchangers","The study of minerals in milk","Microbiology is the study of living organisms of microscopic size."],["Which scientist is associated with the heat-treatment method now called pasteurization?","Louis Pasteur","Robert Koch","Alexander Fleming","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek","The handbook identifies Louis Pasteur with the heat-treatment method called pasteurization."],["How do bacteria normally multiply?","By binary fission","By evaporation","By crystallization","By homogenization","Bacteria normally multiply by binary fission."],["According to Gram staining, what colour are Gram-positive bacteria in the handbook?","Blue","Red","Green","Yellow","The handbook states Gram positives are blue and Gram negatives red."],["What are the three main characteristic bacterial shapes?","Spherical, rod and spiral","Flat, cubic and triangular","Round, square and filament only","Cocci, mould and yeast","The three main characteristic shapes are spherical, rod and spiral."],["Which statement about moulds is correct?","They are normally strictly aerobic","They require no oxygen","They only grow above 60 °C","They cannot grow in acidic media","The handbook describes moulds as strictly aerobic organisms, with a few exceptions."],["What is the optimum growth temperature for most moulds?","20–30 °C","0–4 °C","10–15 °C","60–70 °C","The optimum growth temperature for most moulds is stated as 20–30 °C."],["What are bacteriophages?","Viruses that are bacterial parasites","Milk proteins","Heat-resistant mould spores","Lactic acid molecules","Bacteriophages are viruses and bacterial parasites."],["Why can bacteriophages be a serious problem in fermented dairy production?","They can attack bacteria needed for fermentation","They increase milk fat","They make milk Newtonian","They remove lactose","Phages can infect starter bacteria and cause a starter culture to collapse."],["What is a typical milk pasteurization combination stated in the chapter?","72 °C for 15–20 seconds","45 °C for 30 minutes","65 °C for 2 seconds","100 °C for 1 hour","The chapter gives 72 °C for 15–20 seconds as a typical combination."],["A starter culture performs normally in one batch but suddenly fails in the next despite correct temperature and composition. Which microbiological cause should be considered?","Bacteriophage infection of the starter bacteria","Immediate casein crystallization","Loss of all milk minerals","Excessive Newtonian behavior","Phages can attack specific starter bacteria and cause fermentation failure."],["Why are bacterial spores important in dairy heat processing?","Some spores can survive treatments that kill vegetative cells","Spores are always destroyed below 40 °C","Spores are identical to yeast cells","Spores occur only in packaged milk","Spore resistance is a key reason sterilization requires more severe heat treatment than pasteurization."],["A microorganism grows only when oxygen is present. Which category best fits it?","Aerobic","Anaerobic","Facultatively anaerobic","Bacteriophage","Aerobes require oxygen for growth."],["Why can moulds remain a spoilage risk in acidic dairy foods where many bacteria are inhibited?","Many moulds tolerate acidic conditions and grow at the product surface when oxygen is available","Moulds cannot grow below pH 7","Moulds require sterilization temperatures to multiply","Moulds are not microorganisms","Moulds are important spoilage organisms because acidity alone does not exclude them."],["What is the practical value of Gram staining in dairy microbiology?","It differentiates broad bacterial groups based on cell-wall staining response","It measures milk fat directly","It determines freezing point","It identifies only bacteriophages","Gram reaction is a basic bacterial classification tool."],["Why does lowering temperature slow but not necessarily eliminate microbial risk?","Psychrotrophic organisms can still grow or remain active at low temperatures","All microorganisms die immediately at 4 °C","Cold converts bacteria into viruses","Cooling raises pH above 10","Cold storage controls growth but does not sterilize milk."],["A pasteurized product is later contaminated during filling. Why can shelf life still be poor?","Heat treatment cannot protect against microorganisms introduced after processing","Pasteurization makes later contamination impossible","Recontamination affects only color","Packaging contamination is removed by cooling","Post-process hygiene is critical because pasteurization only acts on organisms present during heating."],["Why is a typical pasteurization process not considered sterilization?","It targets pathogens and reduces spoilage organisms but does not destroy all microbial forms and spores","It uses no heat","It only changes product pH","It removes microorganisms by centrifugation only","Pasteurization is less severe than sterilization and does not create commercial sterility."],["What makes bacteriophage control especially important in starter preparation areas?","Phages replicate in bacteria and can spread through culture handling systems","Phages are removed automatically by lactose fermentation","Phages cannot survive dairy environments","Phages affect only moulds","A contaminated starter system can propagate phage problems rapidly."],["Why can microbial enzymes remain a quality issue even after later chilling?","Damage products and enzymes formed during growth may persist after growth is arrested","Cooling reverses all prior microbial activity","Enzymes exist only above 100 °C","Microbial enzymes cannot affect milk fat or protein","The handbook emphasizes that cooling stops further development but cannot undo prior damage."]]},"5":{"title":"Collection and Reception of Milk","q":[["To what temperature should milk be chilled immediately after milking?","+4 °C","+12 °C","+20 °C","0 °C","The handbook says milk should be chilled to +4 °C immediately after milking."],["What happens if the cold chain is broken during transportation?","Microorganisms can start multiplying","Milk becomes sterile","Protein content increases","All enzymes are destroyed","A break in the cold chain allows microorganisms to multiply and form metabolic products and enzymes."],["What is an example requirement for a farm bulk cooling tank?","Cool all milk to +4 °C within two hours","Heat milk to 72 °C immediately","Keep milk at 20 °C","Remove all milk fat","The chapter gives chilling all milk in the tank to +4 °C within two hours after milking as an example stipulation."],["Why should tanker compartments be well filled?","To prevent sloshing and treat milk gently","To increase aeration","To heat the milk","To increase bacterial growth","Well-filled tanks help prevent milk from sloshing during transport."],["Why is an air eliminator used before volumetric measurement?","To improve measuring accuracy","To pasteurize the milk","To increase fat content","To add oxygen","A flowmeter registers air as well as milk, so removing air improves volume measurement accuracy."],["Which is listed as a common milk-quality test?","Freezing point","Paint viscosity","Pipe wall thickness","Motor current","The handbook lists freezing point among common tests on milk supplies."],["Why is the freezing point of milk checked?","To detect possible dilution with water","To measure bacterial shape","To determine tank pressure","To calculate pump speed","Added water raises the freezing point of milk."],["What is the first thing done at milk reception according to the chapter?","Determine the quantity of milk","Homogenize the milk","Ferment the milk","Dry the milk","At reception, the quantity is determined and recorded."],["Bulk-collected milk can be measured in which two basic ways?","By volume or by weight","By colour or taste","By pH or temperature only","By pressure or viscosity","The chapter describes intake measurement by volume or by weight."],["What is normally done if incoming milk has warmed slightly above +4 °C during transport?","Cool it below +4 °C before silo storage","Warm it to 30 °C","Add water","Leave it at ambient temperature","Incoming milk is usually cooled to below +4 °C in a plate heat exchanger before silo storage."],["A dairy receives milk with entrained air and measures it directly with a volumetric flowmeter. What error is most likely?","The recorded volume is too high because the meter also registers air","The recorded volume is always too low","The milk fat content is measured instead of volume","The freezing point becomes the meter output","Volumetric measurement becomes inaccurate when dispersed air is included."],["Why is gravity transfer preferred into some reception air eliminators instead of pumping directly into the vessel?","It reduces additional air dispersion before measurement","It increases foaming for easier deaeration","It warms the milk for better measurement","It sterilizes the milk before weighing","Reception design aims to avoid introducing more dispersed air."],["A tanker is weighed full and then empty. What quantity is obtained by subtracting tare from gross weight?","The mass of milk delivered","The volume of entrained air","The milk fat percentage","The tanker compartment capacity only","Weighbridge reception determines delivered milk mass from gross minus tare."],["Why are tanker compartments used instead of one very large open volume?","They reduce sloshing and mechanical treatment during transport","They increase oxygen pickup","They eliminate the need for insulation","They make refrigeration unnecessary","Compartments limit movement of milk in transit."],["A tanker arrives after several collection rounds in one day. What cleaning practice follows the handbook?","Clean after each round rather than only once at the end of the day","Clean only once per week","Do not clean if milk stayed below 4 °C","Rinse only the outside of the tanker","Multiple collection rounds justify more frequent internal cleaning."],["Why is freezing-point testing useful for detecting added water?","Added water shifts the freezing point upward toward 0 °C","Added water always lowers freezing point further","Freezing point measures only protein","Added water has no measurable effect","Normal milk freezes below 0 °C; dilution makes the freezing point less negative."],["Why is air elimination positioned before the measuring device?","To improve measurement accuracy by removing a non-product volume fraction","To increase the amount measured","To increase oxygen for preservation","To separate fat from skim milk","The air eliminator prevents entrained air from being counted as milk."],["What is the process reason for cooling incoming milk below about 4 °C again before silo storage?","Transport usually causes some warming, and storage stability depends on restoring low temperature","Cooling increases bacterial growth","Silo storage requires milk above 10 °C","Cooling is needed only for accurate weighing","Reception cooling restores the cold chain before storage."],["Why is a low-level control useful in a reception air eliminator or pumping system?","It prevents the pump from sucking air when the vessel empties","It increases the milk pH","It raises the flowmeter reading deliberately","It prevents all milk from reaching the silo","Level control stops the pump before air is drawn into the line."],["If a dairy wants the most direct mass measurement without volumetric air error, which method is preferable?","Weighing the tanker or a load-cell reception tank","Using an uncompensated volumetric meter","Estimating from pump speed only","Measuring milk temperature only","Weight-based measurement is not affected by entrained air in the same way as volume measurement."]]},"6":{"title":"Building Blocks of Dairy Processing","q":[["Which pump type is most widely used in dairies?","Centrifugal pump","Vacuum ejector","Piston compressor","Gearbox pump","The centrifugal pump is the most widely used dairy pump."],["Which pump type is suitable for gentle treatment and high-viscosity products?","Positive displacement pump","Centrifugal pump only","Vacuum pump","Air blower","Positive displacement pumps are used for gentle treatment and high viscosities."],["What is a common sign of pump cavitation?","A crackling sound","A sweet smell","A blue colour","A higher product pH","Cavitation can be detected by a crackling sound in the pump."],["Why is regenerative heat exchange used in milk pasteurization?","To recover heat from hot pasteurized milk to warm incoming cold milk","To add air to milk","To increase fat content","To eliminate all pumps","Regeneration recovers heat, reducing heating and cooling demand."],["What are the two main tank categories by function?","Storage tanks and process tanks","Hot tanks and cold tanks","Steel tanks and plastic tanks","Open tanks and road tankers","The handbook divides dairy tanks into storage tanks and process tanks."],["Why should a pump be installed close to the source tank?","To minimize suction losses and reduce cavitation risk","To increase air pickup","To raise product temperature","To maximize pipe bends","Short, simple suction lines help maintain adequate inlet pressure."],["Where should a throttling valve normally be installed for a centrifugal pump?","In the delivery line","In the suction line directly before the pump","Inside the balance tank","On the agitator shaft","Flow from a centrifugal pump is normally adjusted on the delivery side."],["What causes cavitation bubbles to form?","Local pressure falling below the liquid vapour pressure","Pressure rising above atmospheric pressure","Milk fat crystallization","High pH","Cavitation starts when local pressure drops below vapour pressure."],["What is the usual purpose of a check valve in a pump delivery line?","To prevent backflow and help protect against water hammer","To add air","To measure viscosity","To increase product temperature","A check valve prevents reverse flow when the pump stops."],["Why are storage tanks insulated?","To help maintain product temperature","To increase oxygen absorption","To promote evaporation","To raise bacterial counts","Insulation reduces heat gain or loss during storage."],["Why can a centrifugal pump perform poorly with heavily aerated milk?","Gas disrupts the continuous liquid phase needed for normal centrifugal pumping","Air increases centrifugal pump efficiency","Aeration always increases NPSH","The pump becomes a positive-displacement pump","Centrifugal pumps are intended mainly for low-viscosity liquids with limited gas content."],["Which pump type is better suited when milk contains a high amount of air?","A liquid-ring pump","A standard centrifugal pump only","A membrane filter","A spray nozzle","The handbook specifically notes liquid-ring pumps for high-air-content duties."],["Why is deaeration before separation and standardization valuable?","Air can reduce separator efficiency and disturb in-line fat standardization","Air improves density measurement","Air makes cream less likely to foam","Air has no effect on downstream equipment","Dispersed air affects separation, standardization and heat transfer."],["In vacuum deaeration, why does milk temperature drop when pressure is reduced?","Part of the water flashes to vapour at the lower boiling point","Fat crystallizes instantly","The milk absorbs heat from the vacuum pump","Lactose decomposes endothermically","Flash evaporation removes heat and lowers product temperature."],["Why must a separator receive a steady flow in an integrated deaeration line?","Stable hydraulic conditions are needed for consistent separation and deaeration","Variable flow improves standardization accuracy","Separators work only with pulsating flow","Flow rate does not affect centrifugal equipment","A flow controller is placed before the separator to maintain constant throughput."],["Why is regenerative heat exchange economically important?","It reuses heat from treated product to preheat incoming product, reducing utility demand","It increases product losses","It eliminates the holding tube","It raises cooling-water temperature with no energy benefit","Regeneration reduces both heating and cooling loads."],["What is the main purpose of a booster pump on the pasteurized side of a regenerative heat exchanger?","Maintain a positive pressure differential to prevent recontamination through leaks","Increase air entrainment","Reduce holding time below specification","Lower pasteurization temperature","The pressure hierarchy ensures leakage flows from pasteurized to unpasteurized side, not the reverse."],["Why is a positive-displacement pump often preferred for a highly viscous dairy product?","Its delivered flow is less dependent on system pressure and it handles high viscosity better","It intentionally aerates the product","It requires no hygienic design","It only works with water-like fluids","Positive-displacement pumps suit viscous and shear-sensitive products."],["Why are service systems considered part of dairy process design rather than separate utilities?","Process capacity and temperature programs determine needs for steam, cooling, air, water and power","Utilities never affect process performance","Only packaging machines use utilities","Utilities are selected after operation without calculation","The handbook treats service demand as integral to plant dimensioning."],["Why must storage-tank agitators avoid excessive shear and air entrainment?","Product quality can be damaged by mechanical treatment and foaming","High shear always improves milk stability","Foam improves silo volume utilization","Air prevents oxidation","Dairy tanks require gentle mixing sufficient for uniformity without unnecessary product damage."]]},"7":{"title":"Designing a Process Line","q":[["Which four requirement groups are highlighted in process design?","Product, process, economic and legal","Colour, taste, weather and transport","Only cost and capacity","Only hygiene and labour","Project engineering balances product-related, process-related, economic and legal requirements."],["What minimum pasteurization combination is stated in the chapter's legal-requirement example?","72 °C for 15 seconds","50 °C for 5 seconds","90 °C for 30 minutes","100 °C for 1 second","The chapter states a minimum of 72 °C for 15 seconds in its summary of common requirements."],["Why should clarification take place before heat treatment?","Particles can shelter bacteria and reduce pasteurization effectiveness","To increase fat globule size","To add oxygen","To reduce milk temperature","Clarification removes solid matter that can protect bacteria during heating."],["What should happen if pasteurization temperature drops too low?","The flow diversion valve returns milk to the balance tank","The product goes directly to filling","More raw milk is added","The holding tube is bypassed","Insufficiently heated milk must be diverted back rather than continue forward."],["Why is higher pressure maintained on the pasteurized side of a heat exchanger?","To prevent untreated milk or service media contaminating pasteurized milk if leakage occurs","To create foam","To increase bacterial growth","To lower the boiling point","The pressure differential is a safeguard against reinfection through leaks."],["What is the typical regenerative energy-saving effect stated for a milk pasteurizer?","About 90–94%","About 10–20%","Exactly 50%","100%","The chapter gives a typical regenerative effect of roughly 90–94%."],["Why is a holding tube dimensioned carefully?","To provide the required holding time at the plant flow rate","To increase cream separation","To add air to the milk","To reduce pipe hygiene","Its length and diameter must ensure the required residence time."],["Why is the pasteurizer balance tank fitted with low-level protection?","To keep the pasteurizer full and prevent burn-on","To increase pressure in the tanker","To improve butter churning","To remove lactose","Low-level control helps avoid running the heat exchanger partially empty."],["At about what temperature is clarification more efficient in the example?","About 55 °C","About 4 °C","About 0 °C","About 90 °C","The handbook notes clarification is more efficient around 55 °C because viscosity is lower."],["What approximate upper product velocity is cited for milk in process piping?","About 3 m/s","About 30 m/s","About 0.03 m/s","About 15 m/s","The example gives about 3 m/s as an upper velocity limit for milk quality considerations."],["A pasteurizer is designed for a fixed holding time. If flow increases above design without changing the holding tube, what happens?","Residence time decreases and required pasteurization may no longer be guaranteed","Residence time increases automatically","Product pressure falls to zero","Regeneration rises to 100%","Holding time depends on flow rate and tube volume."],["Why is a flow controller essential upstream of a holding tube?","It keeps residence time and temperature-control conditions within the designed range","It is used only to measure fat content","It replaces the flow-diversion valve","It eliminates the need for recording temperature","Stable flow is necessary for a known holding time."],["A plate heat exchanger develops a pinhole leak. Why is pasteurized-side pressure kept higher?","So leakage goes from pasteurized milk toward raw milk or service media","So raw milk is forced into the pasteurized side","To increase heat-transfer fouling","To reduce the boiling point","Positive differential pressure is a hygienic safeguard against reinfection."],["Why is hot water normally only a few degrees hotter than the target pasteurization temperature?","A small temperature difference gives controlled heating and limits excessive thermal stress","Steam must never be used in dairies","A large temperature difference would stop heat transfer","Product must remain colder than the heating medium by less than 0.1 °C","The example uses hot water about 2–3 °C above product pasteurization temperature."],["What does a 90–94% regenerative effect mean operationally?","Most of the heating/cooling duty is recovered internally between incoming and outgoing milk","90–94% of the milk is discarded","Only 6–10% of the product is pasteurized","The holding tube is 90–94% full","Regeneration recovers a large share of the thermal duty."],["Why might clarification be placed at about 55 °C within the regenerative section?","Lower viscosity at that temperature improves centrifugal clarification","Milk is sterile at 55 °C","Fat is completely solid at 55 °C","Clarifiers require boiling product","The handbook notes clarification is more efficient at about 55 °C."],["If pasteurization temperature drops below the legal setpoint, why is immediate diversion necessary?","Underprocessed milk must be prevented from mixing with accepted pasteurized product","The product must be sent to packaging quickly","Cooling must be bypassed to save energy","The milk has already become sterile","Diversion separates insufficiently heated product from compliant product."],["Why can an oversized pipe be undesirable even though velocity decreases?","Larger pipes and components increase capital cost and may be unnecessary","Large pipes always cause cavitation","Velocity must always exceed 10 m/s","Large pipes make cleaning impossible","Pipe sizing balances acceptable velocity against cost and equipment size."],["Why is product velocity limited to around 3 m/s in the example?","Higher velocity increases friction and mechanical treatment that may affect product quality","Higher velocity prevents any heat transfer","Higher velocity always causes freezing","Milk cannot physically move faster than 3 m/s","The chapter balances hydraulic performance with gentle product handling."],["Why are pasteurization temperature, chilled-product temperature and diversion-valve position often recorded together?","Together they document whether heating, cooling and safety diversion operated correctly","Only for calculating milk fat","To replace all microbiological testing","To measure tanker weight","Combined records provide a process history for control and verification."]]},"8":{"title":"Pasteurized Milk Products","q":[["Which stages are compulsory in consumer milk processing in most countries according to the handbook?","Clarification, pasteurization and cooling","Fermentation, drying and freezing","Churning and salting","Evaporation only","Clarification, pasteurization and cooling are described as compulsory in most countries."],["What is the purpose of milk standardization?","To give milk a defined, guaranteed fat content","To sterilize packaging","To increase bacterial count","To remove all protein","Standardization controls milk to a specified fat content."],["What is a usual HTST pasteurization range for homogenized milk in this chapter?","72–75 °C for 15–20 seconds","40–45 °C for 2 hours","100 °C for 30 minutes","10 °C for 15 seconds","The handbook gives 72–75 °C for 15–20 seconds as usual for homogenized HTST milk."],["Why can partial homogenization reduce power consumption?","Only a smaller cream stream needs the homogenizer","It eliminates cooling","It removes the need for pasteurization","It increases tanker capacity","Partial homogenization can use a smaller homogenizer while maintaining the desired effect."],["What does the flow diversion valve do if pasteurization temperature drops?","Returns milk to the balance tank","Sends milk to the filler","Adds cream","Stops all cooling water permanently","A low pasteurization temperature triggers diversion back to the balance tank."],["What is the purpose of homogenization in market milk?","To reduce fat-globule size and prevent creaming","To remove lactose","To sterilize the milk","To raise the freezing point","Homogenization reduces fat globule size so cream separation is reduced."],["What NIZO value example is given for pasteurized milk?","About 70%","About 10%","About 30%","About 100%","The chapter gives around 70% as an example requirement for pasteurized milk."],["What NIZO value example is given for ESL milk?","About 80%","About 20%","About 40%","About 60%","The chapter gives around 80% as an example for ESL milk."],["Why should packaged pasteurized milk be protected from light?","Light can damage nutrients and create off-flavour","Light increases pasteurization efficiency","Light raises fat content","Light prevents oxidation","Exposure to light can degrade nutrients and contribute to sunlight flavour."],["Which amino acid is associated with sunlight flavour formation in milk?","Methionine","Lysine","Glycine","Alanine","Light can degrade methionine to methional, producing a characteristic off-flavour."],["Why is cream diluted to about 18% fat before partial homogenization in the example?","Very high-fat cream is unsuitable for efficient homogenization and is therefore diluted with skim milk","The homogenizer can only process skim milk","Dilution destroys all fat globules","Cream must be below 1% fat for homogenization","The handbook limits the fat concentration entering the homogenizer in the partial system."],["Why does partial homogenization reduce equipment size?","Only the cream fraction needed for standardization passes through the homogenizer","The whole plant operates at lower flow","Pasteurization is omitted","The separator is removed","Treating only a fraction of total milk reduces homogenizer capacity requirements."],["Why is the homogenized cream recombined with skim milk before pasteurization?","To reach the final target fat content in standardized market milk","To create an aseptic product without heating","To increase the cream to 40% fat","To remove lactose","The streams are recombined to produce the specified final composition."],["If homogenization efficiency is too low, what visible defect is most likely during storage?","Creaming or formation of a cream layer","Casein precipitation at pH 4.6","Instant lactose crystallization","Complete whey separation from all milk","Homogenization is intended to prevent or reduce cream separation."],["Why is light exposure particularly problematic after homogenization?","The dispersed fat and sensitive nutrients are more exposed to oxidation and photochemical reactions","Homogenization makes milk opaque to all light","Light raises pasteurization temperature","Light sterilizes packaged milk","The chapter emphasizes light protection, especially for homogenized milk."],["Why is a booster pump included in the market-milk pasteurizer?","To keep pasteurized milk at higher pressure than raw milk or cooling media in the heat exchanger","To aerate the milk before filling","To reduce fat content","To increase bacterial count","The booster pump protects against contamination if heat-exchanger plates leak."],["A plant wants a longer-shelf-life milk and targets a higher NIZO value. What does the higher value indicate?","More effective homogenization and lower tendency to cream","Higher bacterial count","Lower pasteurization temperature","More entrained air","NIZO value is used as a measure of homogenization efficiency."],["Why is final cooling after pasteurization done partly regeneratively?","Heat from outgoing pasteurized milk can preheat incoming raw milk","Cold milk must be mixed with hot milk","Regeneration increases bacterial growth","Ice water cannot be used in dairies","Regeneration recovers heat and reduces utility use."],["In the Scania whipping-cream method, why is the holding tank intentionally not agitated?","To minimize mechanical damage and allow natural deaeration","To increase foaming","To increase free-fat formation","To prevent lipase inactivation","The method is designed around very gentle handling of cream."],["Why can too low a cooling temperature be risky for high-fat whipping cream in a heat exchanger?","Rapid viscosity increase can raise pressure drop and damage fat globules","It always lowers viscosity","It makes the cream sterile","It prevents any fat crystallization","Higher-fat cream may need a higher cooling temperature to avoid excessive viscosity and mechanical damage."]]},"9":{"title":"Long-Life Milk","q":[["What is integral to UHT processing to prevent reinfection after treatment?","Aseptic filling","Open-tank storage","Air injection","Manual bottle rinsing only","Aseptic filling is an integral part of the UHT process."],["Which are the two alternative UHT heating approaches described?","Indirect heat exchange and direct steam heating","Freezing and drying","Churning and salting","Centrifugation and filtration only","UHT treatment can use indirect heat exchangers or direct steam injection/infusion."],["What is the typical temperature range stated for in-container sterilization?","115–120 °C","40–50 °C","65–70 °C","200–220 °C","The handbook states in-container sterilization is usually at 115–120 °C."],["About how long is in-container sterilization typically held?","20–30 minutes","1–2 seconds","2 hours","24 hours","The stated typical holding time is about 20–30 minutes."],["Why must aseptic handling follow UHT treatment?","To avoid reinfection of the commercially sterilized product","To increase lactose","To create cream separation","To reduce packaging strength","Long shelf life depends on preventing recontamination after heat treatment."],["Why is UHT treatment followed by aseptic packaging?","To prevent reinfection after sterilizing treatment","To increase cream separation","To reduce protein content","To promote fermentation","The long shelf life depends on preventing post-process contamination."],["What direct UHT methods are mentioned?","Steam injection or infusion","Only plate cooling","Only centrifugation","Only membrane filtration","Direct systems heat product by steam injection or infusion."],["What indirect UHT equipment is mentioned?","Plate or tubular heat exchangers","Only open kettles","Only spray dryers","Only butter churns","Indirect systems transfer heat through heat-exchanger surfaces."],["What is the key difference between UHT and in-container sterilization?","UHT uses much shorter high-temperature treatment followed by aseptic filling","UHT uses no heat","In-container sterilization is always colder than refrigeration","There is no difference","UHT achieves sterilizing effect with high temperature for short time and aseptic handling."],["What must happen to equipment downstream of the UHT heating step?","It must remain aseptic","It must be open to air","It must be operated warm only","It must contain raw milk","Downstream equipment must preserve aseptic conditions to avoid recontamination."],["Why can upstream homogenization be used in an indirect UHT plant without aseptic homogenizer design?","The product is homogenized before the final sterilizing heat treatment","Indirect systems are always sterile before startup","Homogenization itself sterilizes the product","There is no post-homogenization heating","Final UHT heating can sterilize microorganisms introduced at upstream homogenization."],["Why might an aseptic downstream homogenizer still be selected?","It can improve physical stability of high-protein, high-solids or high-fat products after UHT heating","It lowers the UHT temperature to pasteurization levels","It removes all minerals","It makes aseptic filling unnecessary","The handbook notes downstream aseptic homogenization can improve texture and stability."],["In direct steam injection, why is a vacuum vessel used after heating?","To flash off the added steam and rapidly cool the product","To add more water to the milk","To separate cream from skim milk","To increase product residence time for minutes","The vacuum step removes the condensed steam and returns product temperature toward the preheating level."],["Why must steam used for direct UHT contact be suitable for food contact?","It becomes part of the product before being flashed off","Steam never contacts the product","Steam is used only outside the heat exchanger","Any boiler steam is acceptable regardless of quality","Direct heating puts steam in direct contact with food."],["Why is uniform droplet size important in steam infusion?","Heat-transfer rate depends on droplet geometry, so uneven droplets receive uneven treatment","Droplet size controls only color","Larger droplets always heat faster","Droplet size has no thermal effect","Infusion design assumes consistent droplet behavior."],["An indirect UHT plant heats product to about 137 °C for roughly four seconds. What is the design objective?","Achieve commercial sterility with limited quality damage through high temperature and short time","Pasteurize without affecting spores","Freeze the product before filling","Promote starter culture growth","UHT combines high temperature and short holding time to balance lethality and quality."],["If product temperature falls below specification during UHT production, why is the line typically flushed and re-sterilized?","Aseptic integrity and required lethality can no longer be assured","The product becomes too viscous only","The homogenizer automatically repairs the problem","Cooling water sterilizes the plant","An under-temperature event compromises the validated sterile process state."],["Why can indirect UHT be more energy-efficient than direct systems in some applications?","Heat regeneration through exchanger walls can recover a large fraction of energy","Indirect systems use no heat","Direct systems cannot cool product","Indirect systems require no pumps","Indirect systems are well suited to regenerative heat recovery."],["Why is aseptic filling inseparable from UHT shelf-life performance?","Commercially sterile product can be spoiled if recontaminated during packaging","Packaging has no influence after UHT","Aseptic filling is only for appearance","UHT milk is immune to all later contamination","Post-process contamination would negate the sterilizing treatment."],["Why might double homogenization be used for products such as coffee cream or evaporated milk?","Upstream and downstream homogenization can improve premium texture and long-term physical stability","It is required to raise bacterial count","It removes the need for UHT heating","It doubles fat content","The handbook cites double homogenization for selected products needing high storage stability."]]},"10":{"title":"Cultures and Starter Manufacture","q":[["What do starter bacteria convert lactose into?","Lactic acid","Milk fat","Oxygen","Calcium phosphate","Starter bacteria ferment lactose, producing lactic acid."],["What effect does the drop in pH during fermentation have?","A preservative effect","It sterilizes the product instantly","It removes all water","It increases oxygen solubility only","The pH drop has a preservative effect on the product."],["Lactic acid bacteria are described as what regarding oxygen?","Facultatively anaerobic","Strictly aerobic only","Strictly anaerobic only","Unable to grow in milk","The handbook describes lactic acid bacteria as facultatively anaerobic."],["Which carbon source do lactic acid bacteria prefer?","Lactose","Cellulose","Mineral salts","Oxygen","Lactic acid bacteria prefer lactose as a carbon source."],["What distinguishes heterofermentative fermentation?","Products other than lactic acid can also form","Only lactic acid is formed","No acid is formed","The bacteria form spores","Heterofermentation can also produce substances such as acetic acid, carbon dioxide and ethanol."],["Do lactic acid bacteria form spores according to the chapter?","No","Yes, all of them","Only during cooling","Only in yoghurt","The chapter states lactic acid bacteria do not form spores."],["Most lactic acid bacteria are killed at approximately what temperature?","Around 70 °C","Around 4 °C","Around 20 °C","Around 150 °C","Most are killed by heating to around 70 °C, though some require more."],["What does homofermentative fermentation mainly produce?","Lactic acid","Only ethanol","Only carbon dioxide","Milk fat","Homofermentative cultures produce almost exclusively lactic acid."],["Why do lactic acid bacteria need organic nitrogen compounds?","For growth","To form milk fat","To increase oxygen content","To crystallize lactose","They obtain organic nitrogen by breaking down casein with proteolytic enzymes."],["Why are different starter cultures used for different dairy products?","Different products require different acidity, flavour, aroma and consistency","All cultures behave identically","Only colour differs","Starter selection has no product effect","Culture choice is matched to the desired product characteristics."],["A culture produces mainly lactic acid with little else. How is this fermentation classified?","Homofermentative","Heterofermentative","Proteolytic only","Aerobic respiration only","Homofermentative lactic acid bacteria produce primarily lactic acid."],["A culture also produces acetic acid, carbon dioxide and ethanol. What does that indicate?","Heterofermentative metabolism","Pure homofermentation","No lactose fermentation","Spore formation","Heterofermentation yields lactic acid plus other end products."],["Why is starter propagation vulnerable to bacteriophages?","The system contains dense populations of the exact bacterial hosts phages need to replicate","Phages grow only in sterile water","Starter bacteria destroy all viruses instantly","Phages infect only moulds","High concentrations of susceptible bacteria make culture systems vulnerable."],["Why are thermophilic cultures chosen for some products rather than mesophilic cultures?","Their optimum growth range better matches higher fermentation temperatures","They always produce less acid","They cannot ferment lactose","They are resistant to all bacteriophages","Culture choice must match product process temperature and desired properties."],["Why can excessive heat treatment of starter milk be useful before inoculation?","It reduces competing microorganisms and can improve the growth medium for the starter","It eliminates lactose needed by the culture","It guarantees all phages are destroyed under any condition","It removes all nitrogen compounds","Starter preparation requires a clean, favorable substrate for the selected culture."],["What is the practical reason for selecting mixed-strain or defined cultures carefully?","Different organisms contribute different rates of acidification, aroma and texture","All strains are functionally identical","Only color is affected","Culture composition has no process consequence","Starter composition determines product characteristics and process performance."],["Why does pH continue to fall during culture growth?","Lactose is metabolized to organic acids, especially lactic acid","Fat is converted directly to calcium salts","Water evaporates from the tank","Protein becomes lactose","Acid production during fermentation lowers pH."],["Why can a starter culture improve preservation even without a final sterilization step?","Acidification creates conditions less favorable for many spoilage and pathogenic organisms","It removes all oxygen from packaging","It raises pH above neutral","It destroys all spores","The acid environment contributes to product preservation."],["Why do lactic acid bacteria need proteolytic activity in milk?","Milk contains limited free amino acids, so bacteria may need to release nitrogen compounds from casein","Proteolysis produces milk fat","It is required to increase lactose concentration","Proteolysis sterilizes the product","Growth requires organic nitrogen, and some species obtain it by breaking down casein."],["Why is starter activity often monitored through acidification behavior?","Rate and extent of pH decrease reflect culture metabolic performance","pH measures only fat content","Acidification is unrelated to fermentation","pH cannot change during culture growth","Acid development is a direct outcome of lactose fermentation."]]},"11":{"title":"Fermented Milk Products","q":[["What converts part of the lactose to lactic acid in fermented milk?","Starter culture","A separator","A homogenizer alone","A vacuum pump","Fermented milk is inoculated with a starter culture that converts lactose to lactic acid."],["What heat treatment is stated as giving optimum yoghurt milk results?","90–95 °C for about five minutes","40 °C for 10 seconds","65 °C for one second","120 °C for one hour","The handbook states optimum results at 90–95 °C with about five minutes holding."],["Why is yoghurt milk heat treated before inoculation?","To improve culture substrate and coagulum stability and reduce whey separation","To add oxygen","To remove all lactose","To freeze the product","The heat treatment improves culture conditions, firmness and stability."],["What is a common stabilizer for drinking yoghurt?","Pectin","Sodium chloride only","Rennet","Calcium metal","Pectin is commonly used to avoid sedimentation and whey separation."],["To about what temperature is set yoghurt eventually cooled in cold storage?","5 °C","25 °C","45 °C","90 °C","The chapter states set yoghurt is eventually cooled to about 5 °C."],["What homogenization range is generally recommended for yoghurt milk?","20–25 MPa at about 65–70 °C","1 MPa at 4 °C","100 MPa at 0 °C","No homogenization is ever used","The handbook gives 20–25 MPa and 65–70 °C as a general recommendation."],["What percentage of whey proteins is roughly denatured by 90–95 °C for five minutes?","About 70–80%","About 5%","About 20%","100% of all milk protein","The stated heat treatment denatures around 70–80% of whey proteins."],["Which whey protein particularly interacts with kappa-casein during yoghurt heat treatment?","Beta-lactoglobulin","Lactose","Casein fines","Milk fat","Beta-lactoglobulin interaction with kappa-casein helps strengthen yoghurt body."],["What two broad methods can extend fermented-milk shelf life?","Aseptic/high-hygiene production and heat treatment","Only freezing and drying","Only adding salt","Only centrifugation","The chapter describes aseptic production and heat treatment as two routes to longer shelf life."],["Why is set yoghurt protected from mechanical disturbance late in incubation?","To reduce the risk of whey separation","To increase oxygen absorption","To prevent lactose formation","To raise fat content","The gel is sensitive during the later incubation period and disturbance can cause syneresis."],["Why does heating yoghurt milk to about 90–95 °C for several minutes improve final gel stability?","Denatured whey proteins interact with casein and strengthen the network","It removes all milk protein","It prevents any fermentation","It crystallizes all lactose","Heat-induced whey-protein interactions improve body and reduce whey separation."],["Why is homogenization usually performed before fermentation rather than after a set gel has formed?","It improves fat dispersion and physical stability without mechanically breaking the finished gel","Homogenization cannot process liquid milk","It is needed only to increase pH","Post-fermentation homogenization always increases gel firmness","Mechanical treatment after gel formation can damage structure."],["A set yoghurt is disturbed mechanically late in incubation. What defect becomes more likely?","Whey separation due to damage of the fragile gel","Higher fat content","Lower bacterial count immediately","Complete lactose removal","Set yoghurt becomes increasingly sensitive to disturbance as the gel forms."],["Why is heat treatment of finished yoghurt able to extend shelf life but change its legal/product identity in some countries?","Heating inactivates the live starter organisms that define yoghurt in some standards","Heating increases starter activity","Heating makes the product raw","Heating turns lactose into fat","Some definitions require viable yoghurt organisms."],["Why is pectin useful in drinking yoghurt?","It helps prevent sedimentation and whey separation and improves mouthfeel","It acts as the starter culture","It replaces all milk proteins","It lowers fat by centrifugation","Pectin is used as a stabilizer in drinking yoghurt."],["Why can homogenization improve the effect of pectin in drinking yoghurt?","Mechanical dispersion helps the stabilizer interact uniformly with the product","Homogenization destroys pectin completely","It increases product pH to neutral","It removes water","The handbook notes mechanical treatment improves pectin stabilization."],["A yoghurt intended for ambient storage is heat treated after fermentation. What additional packaging requirement becomes critical?","Aseptic filling to prevent reinfection","Open filling to release pressure","No cleaning of the filler","Packaging at farm temperature","Once microorganisms are inactivated, recontamination must be prevented."],["Why does concentrated yoghurt have a higher protein content than ordinary yoghurt?","Whey removal after fermentation increases dry matter and protein concentration","Extra water is added after fermentation","Protein is created by cooling","Fat is converted into casein","Concentration removes part of the serum phase."],["Why is post-acidification undesirable during refrigerated yoghurt storage?","Continued culture activity can make the product too acidic and alter flavor/texture","It always increases shelf life without quality effects","It raises pH","It prevents whey separation completely","Excess continued acid development can degrade sensory quality."],["Why does fermentation temperature need to match the selected culture?","Culture growth rate and metabolite production depend strongly on temperature","Temperature affects only packaging","All starters have identical optimum temperatures","Fermentation occurs independently of temperature","Starter cultures are selected and operated within appropriate temperature ranges."]]},"12":{"title":"Butter and Dairy Spreads","q":[["A fat spread is mainly what type of emulsion?","Water-in-oil","Oil-in-water only","Gas-in-solid","Solid-in-gas","The IDF definition describes fat spreads mainly as water-in-oil emulsions."],["What should wrapping material for butter resist?","Grease, light, flavour/aroma transfer and moisture","Only pressure","Only cold","Only oxygen, with no other requirements","Butter wrapping should be greaseproof and protect against light, aromas and moisture loss."],["What material has largely replaced parchment paper for butter wrapping?","Aluminium foil","Cardboard alone","Glass","Cotton","The handbook notes aluminium foil has largely replaced parchment paper."],["At what temperature should packed butter and dairy spreads be kept in cold storage?","+5 °C","+25 °C","−25 °C always","+40 °C","The chapter recommends cold storage at +5 °C for consistency and appearance."],["In the spread process, where does phase inversion take place?","Scraped-surface coolers","Road tanker","Cheese vat","Spray dryer","The process line describes phase inversion in scraped-surface coolers."],["What are the two main blocks of the dairy-spread process line?","A dairy block and a margarine block","A cheese block and a whey block","A powder block and an ice block","A tanker block and a silo block","The spread process is organized around a dairy block and a margarine-type block."],["To what range is cream adjusted before concentration in the dairy block?","About 60–70 °C","About 0–4 °C","About 20–25 °C","About 100–120 °C","The cream temperature is adjusted to roughly 60–70 °C before the concentrator."],["What fat level can concentrated cream reach in the process described?","About 82%","About 10%","About 35%","About 99.8%","The standard process can concentrate cream to around 82% fat."],["What equipment works the spread before final cooling?","Pin rotors","A milk clarifier","A spray dryer","A road tanker","Pin rotors work the product between cooling stages."],["Why must butter packaging be moisture-impermeable?","To prevent surface drying and colour change","To increase fermentation","To remove fat","To increase water loss deliberately","Moisture loss dries the surface and can make outer layers more yellow."],["During butter manufacture, why is cream fat crystallization before churning important?","The proportion and form of solid fat strongly influence churning and butter consistency","All fat must remain fully liquid","Crystallization sterilizes the cream","Crystallization removes water","Controlled fat crystallization is central to butter texture and phase inversion."],["What fundamental emulsion change occurs when cream becomes butter?","Oil-in-water cream is converted toward a water-in-oil structure","Water-in-oil becomes oil-in-water","Protein solution becomes a gas foam","All water is evaporated","Butter manufacture involves phase inversion of the fat/water system."],["Why is working the butter after churning important?","It distributes moisture and develops a uniform plastic structure","It increases bacterial growth deliberately","It removes all fat","It raises moisture without limit","Working controls water dispersion and body."],["Why must excessive mechanical treatment of whipping cream be avoided before use?","It can damage fat globules and create free fat or pre-churning","It always improves whipping","It sterilizes the cream","It eliminates lipase","Whipping cream quality depends on maintaining fat-globule integrity."],["Why is butter wrapping required to be light-impermeable?","Light can promote oxidation and flavor deterioration","Light melts lactose","Light lowers the freezing point","Light improves fat crystallization","Protection from light preserves butter quality."],["Why are scraped-surface coolers used in dairy-spread manufacture?","They provide controlled cooling of viscous fat systems while maintaining heat transfer","They separate cream by density","They measure pH","They are used only for raw milk storage","Viscous fat-rich products require continuous cooling with surface renewal."],["What is the role of pin rotors in spread manufacture?","They work and structure the product between cooling stages","They pasteurize the product","They remove air from tankers","They determine freezing point","Pin rotors provide mechanical working during crystallization."],["Why does fat composition influence butter hardness?","Different fatty acids and triglycerides have different melting behavior","Hardness depends only on lactose","All milk fat has one melting point","Protein concentration determines all fat crystallization","Milk fat is a mixture with a broad melting range."],["Why is cold storage around +5 °C recommended after packaging?","It helps stabilize consistency and appearance","It guarantees sterility","It keeps all milk fat fully liquid","It promotes rapid oxidation","Cold storage preserves the physical quality of butter and spreads."],["What is one process advantage of the NIZO butter method mentioned in the handbook?","It allows easier utilization of sweet buttermilk","It eliminates all starter cultures","It makes cooling unnecessary","It converts butter directly to AMF","The NIZO approach uses sweet cream and facilitates use of sweet buttermilk."]]},"13":{"title":"Anhydrous Milk Fat (AMF) and Butteroil","q":[["What minimum milk-fat content is specified for anhydrous milk fat?","99.8%","82%","50%","95%","The chapter states AMF must contain at least 99.8% milk fat."],["What are the two principal raw-material routes for AMF production?","Cream and reworked butter","Whey and yoghurt","Skim milk and water only","Cheese and casein","AMF is principally produced directly from cream or from reworked butter."],["At approximately what temperature is AMF liquid?","Above 36 °C","Above 5 °C","Only above 100 °C","Below 0 °C","The handbook states AMF is liquid above about 36 °C."],["Cream entering an AMF plant is typically what fat content?","35–40%","1–2%","10%","99.8%","The cream route begins with cream at roughly 35–40% fat."],["To about what fat level is cream pre-concentrated in the first centrifuge?","75%","10%","25%","99.9%","The cream is pre-concentrated to about 75% fat before further processing."],["What fat content does the final concentrator produce before final drying in the cream route?","About 99.5%","About 75%","About 40%","About 10%","The final concentrator produces a light phase of about 99.5% fat."],["Why is a homogenizer used in the AMF cream process?","For phase inversion and release of fat from globules","To pasteurize the product","To add water","To crystallize lactose","The homogenizer disrupts fat globules to invert the phase before final concentration."],["What moisture content is targeted after vacuum treatment?","Not more than about 0.1%","About 10%","About 25%","About 50%","Vacuum treatment reduces moisture to approximately 0.1% or less."],["What is a typical AMF packing temperature?","About 40 °C","About 4 °C","About 90 °C","About −20 °C","The handbook gives roughly 40 °C as a typical packing temperature."],["Why can direct steam melting of butter cause cloudy AMF?","It can create a difficult emulsion with small air bubbles","It removes too much protein","It freezes the butter","It lowers the fat content to zero","Direct steam can form a dispersed phase that is hard to separate and causes cloudiness."],["Why is cream pre-concentrated before final AMF separation?","Removing much of the aqueous phase first improves efficiency of final fat concentration","Pre-concentration adds water","It lowers fat content to skim-milk levels","It is done only for flavor","The process raises cream from about 35–40% to roughly 75% fat before final concentration."],["Why is phase inversion necessary before the final concentrator in the cream route?","Fat must become the continuous phase so residual serum can be separated efficiently","It increases lactose solubility","It converts fat to protein","It sterilizes the product","Homogenization disrupts fat globules and releases fat for final concentration."],["Why is a vacuum chamber used near the end of AMF production?","To remove residual moisture to very low levels","To increase protein content","To add steam permanently","To crystallize lactose","Vacuum drying reduces moisture to around 0.1% or below."],["Why can aged butter yield a clearer AMF than freshly churned butter?","Fresh butter can be harder to split completely into phases","Aged butter has no fat","Fresh butter contains no water","Ageing removes all protein chemically","The handbook notes fresh butter emulsions can be more difficult to break and may give cloudy oil."],["Why is direct steam melting of butter generally avoided before AMF concentration?","It can create a stable dispersed phase with air/steam effects that is hard to separate","It freezes the butter","It reduces fat to zero","It always burns the butter instantly","Direct steam can create troublesome emulsions and cloudiness."],["Why is AMF convenient for recombination plants?","It is concentrated, easy to meter in liquid form and occupies less storage space than butter","It contains more water than butter","It needs fermentation before use","It is always solid at room temperature","AMF is a compact, pumpable source of milk fat."],["What is the purpose of polishing or washing AMF oil?","To improve quality when raw material or intermediate oil contains unwanted impurities","To increase water content","To add casein","To produce yoghurt","Refining steps can improve AMF quality before final evaporation."],["Why must AMF intended to meet the strict Codex definition be made without neutralizing additives?","The standard for anhydrous milk fat does not allow neutralization of free fatty acids","Neutralizers increase fat content too much","Neutralizers are required only below 50% fat","AMF contains no fatty acids","The handbook distinguishes AMF from anhydrous butteroil partly by permitted neutralization."],["What does fractionation of AMF accomplish?","It separates milk fat into fractions with different melting and functional properties","It separates lactose from casein","It pasteurizes the fat","It adds vegetable oil","Fractionation is used to tailor fat functionality."],["Why is nitrogen headspace used in AMF drums?","To reduce oxygen exposure and oxidative deterioration","To increase moisture","To promote lipolysis","To cool the fat below 0 °C","Excluding oxygen improves storage stability of concentrated milk fat."]]},"14":{"title":"Cheese","q":[["What is thermization as described for cheese milk?","65 °C for 15 seconds followed by cooling to about +4 °C","90 °C for 30 minutes","Freezing at −20 °C","Boiling for one hour","Thermization is a moderate heat treatment of about 65 °C for 15 seconds followed by cooling."],["Why is cheese milk generally not homogenized for semi-hard and hard cheese?","It greatly increases water binding and can increase losses in whey","It destroys all lactose","It makes milk impossible to heat","It removes calcium completely","Homogenization can make moisture control difficult and increase fat and dry-solids losses."],["Why should air pickup be avoided when filling a cheese tank?","It can affect coagulum quality and increase fat/casein losses","It improves coagulation too much","It sterilizes the milk","It reduces starter activity to zero","The handbook warns that air pickup can harm coagulum quality and increase losses."],["What is the typical coagulation time stated?","About 30 minutes","30 seconds","5 hours","24 hours","Renneting/coagulation time is typically about 30 minutes."],["How does finer curd cutting affect final cheese moisture?","It generally lowers moisture","It always raises moisture","It has no effect","It eliminates whey instantly","The finer the curd is cut, the lower the moisture content of the resulting cheese."],["Why may old cheese milk be thermized before storage?","To arrest growth of psychrotrophic flora","To increase fat content","To remove casein","To add oxygen","Thermization is used to limit psychrotrophic growth during additional storage."],["What pre-ripening time is stated after starter addition?","About 30–60 minutes","About 30 seconds","About 8 hours","About 24 hours","The chapter gives about 30–60 minutes for starter acclimatization before active growth."],["How long should milk be stirred after rennet addition at most?","Not more than about 5 minutes","At least 30 minutes","Two hours","It must never be stirred","The handbook recommends careful stirring for no more than about five minutes after rennet addition."],["What is a typical curd-grain size range after cutting?","About 3–15 mm","About 3–15 cm","Less than 0.1 mm","More than 10 cm","Curd is cut into grains typically around 3–15 mm depending on cheese type."],["Why is calcium chloride sometimes added before rennet?","To support coagulation when needed","To sweeten the milk","To increase fat content","To sterilize the vat","Calcium chloride can be added to improve coagulation properties."],["Why can 24-hour cold storage at about 5 °C temporarily impair cheesemaking properties?","Calcium phosphate precipitates and some beta-casein leaves the micelle","All lactose is fermented","Milk fat becomes permanently oxidized","The milk becomes sterile","Cold storage shifts protein/mineral equilibria important for coagulation."],["Why can pasteurization partly restore coagulation properties after cold storage?","Heat reverses some cold-induced mineral and casein changes","Pasteurization adds calcium chloride automatically","Heat creates new casein from lactose","Pasteurization removes all whey proteins","The handbook notes calcium redissolution and recovery of micellar behavior."],["Why is Pseudomonas growth in cold-stored cheese milk particularly undesirable?","Its proteolytic and lipolytic enzymes can create bitter and rancid defects","It improves rennet coagulation","It produces only desirable aroma","It increases calcium availability","Psychrotroph enzymes degrade protein and fat even under cold-storage conditions."],["Why should air pickup be minimized when filling a cheese vat?","Foam and air can disturb coagulum formation and increase fat/casein losses to whey","Air improves curd strength","Air reduces whey losses","Air replaces starter culture","Gentle filling supports uniform coagulation and yield."],["Why is starter often added while the vat is filling?","It improves distribution and gives cells time to acclimatize before coagulation","Starter must be added after pressing","It prevents any acid development","It immediately separates whey","Early dosing improves culture distribution and pre-ripening."],["Why must stirring stop soon after rennet addition?","The forming coagulum needs to become still to avoid casein losses and weak gel formation","Continuous agitation is required for coagulation","Rennet works only under turbulent flow","Stopping stirring prevents acid formation","The milk should come to rest before the gel sets."],["Why does finer cutting of curd usually produce a drier cheese?","Smaller grains have more surface area for whey expulsion","Fine cutting traps more water","Grain size affects only color","Fine cutting stops syneresis","Smaller curd particles lose whey more readily."],["Why might calcium chloride be added to cheese milk?","To improve coagulation when available ionic calcium is insufficient","To sweeten the cheese","To sterilize raw milk","To increase lactose","Calcium chloride can support rennet coagulation."],["What is the process purpose of cooking or heating curd after cutting?","To control syneresis, moisture and microbial/enzymatic development","To remelt fat completely","To eliminate all starter organisms in every cheese","To increase whey protein content in curd","Curd temperature is a major control of whey expulsion and cheese texture."],["Why does thermization not replace pasteurization when pasteurization is legally required?","Thermization is a milder treatment intended mainly to control psychrotrophs during storage","Thermization is always more severe than UHT","Thermization sterilizes milk completely","Thermization has no effect on microorganisms","Thermization is moderate heat treatment, not the same safety process as pasteurization."]]},"15":{"title":"Whey Processing","q":[["Whey is the liquid residue from which processes?","Cheese, casein and yoghurt production","Only butter packaging","Only milk reception","Only ice-cream hardening","The chapter defines whey as liquid residue from cheese, casein and yoghurt production."],["Approximately what share of the original milk nutrients does whey contain?","About 50%","About 1%","About 90%","None","The handbook states whey contains about 50% of the nutrients in the original milk."],["What pH range is given for sweet whey?","5.9–6.6","2.0–3.0","7.5–8.5","9.0–10.0","Sweet whey from cheese/rennet casein is stated to have pH 5.9–6.6."],["Why should casein fines be removed before fat separation?","They adversely affect fat separation","They increase pasteurization temperature","They sterilize whey","They increase air content","Casein fines interfere with fat separation and are therefore removed first."],["For short whey storage under 8 hours, what is usually sufficient?","Chilling to below 5 °C","Holding at 30 °C","Boiling continuously","Adding air","Chilling below 5 °C is usually sufficient to reduce bacterial activity for short storage."],["What dry-matter range can RO/NF typically reach in the first concentration step?","About 18–25%","About 1–2%","About 60–80%","About 100%","The chapter describes membrane concentration from around 6% to roughly 18–25% DM."],["Above what dry-matter level does MVR evaporation become more economical?","About 25–30%","About 1%","About 5%","About 90%","The text states MVR evaporation becomes more economical above roughly 25–30% DM."],["Why is concentrated whey crystallized before spray drying?","To form small lactose crystals and reduce hygroscopicity","To remove all proteins","To increase bacterial growth","To add fat","Controlled lactose crystallization helps produce a less hygroscopic powder."],["How long may whey concentrate be held in crystallizers according to the example?","About 4–8 hours","About 10 seconds","About 24–48 hours","About 1 minute","The chapter gives a holding time of roughly 4–8 hours for crystal development."],["What fat content is typical for recovered whey cream?","About 25–30%","About 1%","About 70%","About 99.8%","Recovered whey cream is described as typically containing around 25–30% fat."],["Why are casein fines removed before whey fat separation?","Fines interfere with efficient centrifugal separation and can increase losses","They increase fat density beneficially","They sterilize the separator","They dissolve all lactose","Removing solids improves separator performance."],["Why is whey often pasteurized soon after cheese manufacture?","Its nutrients support rapid microbial growth if not stabilized","Whey contains no microorganisms","Pasteurization is needed only to change color","Whey is naturally sterile","Fresh whey is microbiologically perishable."],["Why is reverse osmosis attractive as an early concentration step?","It removes water with lower thermal energy input than evaporation","It selectively removes only fat","It sterilizes whey","It increases lactose content by chemical reaction","Membrane concentration can reduce evaporator energy load."],["Why does evaporation become more suitable after membrane concentration reaches higher dry matter?","Membranes become less economical at high concentration while evaporation handles further water removal effectively","Evaporation cannot process concentrated streams","RO becomes more efficient as osmotic pressure rises indefinitely","Dry matter has no effect on process choice","The handbook combines membrane and evaporation steps for economy."],["Why is lactose crystallized before drying concentrated whey?","Controlled crystallization reduces stickiness and hygroscopicity in the dryer and powder","Crystallization removes all protein","It increases whey fat","It sterilizes the powder","Amorphous lactose contributes to sticky powder behavior."],["Why must crystallization be controlled rather than allowed randomly?","Crystal size and degree of crystallization affect drying behavior and final powder properties","Any crystal size gives identical powder","Crystallization controls only color","Lactose does not crystallize in whey","Controlled conditions produce more predictable handling."],["What does ultrafiltration primarily retain in whey?","Proteins and larger molecules while much lactose and minerals pass into permeate","Only water molecules","Only milk fat globules","All minerals and lactose","UF is used to produce whey protein concentrates by selective membrane retention."],["Why can whey protein concentrate have a much higher protein percentage than raw whey?","Water, lactose and minerals are selectively removed while proteins are retained and concentrated","Protein is chemically created during filtration","Fat is converted into protein","All casein is added from cheese curd","Membrane fractionation increases the relative protein content."],["Why is recovered whey cream often treated separately from the remaining whey?","Fat recovery creates a valuable co-product and reduces fat load in later whey processing","Fat improves every membrane process","Separating fat lowers protein content to zero","Whey cream is waste only","Fat recovery improves both value and downstream processability."],["Why is short-term whey storage normally kept below about 5 °C?","To slow microbial growth before further processing","To accelerate fermentation","To keep lactose fully crystallized","To increase fat separation by warming","Cold storage stabilizes whey between process steps."]]},"16":{"title":"Condensed Milk","q":[["What are the two main types of condensed milk described?","Unsweetened and sweetened","Raw and fermented","Frozen and dried","Blue and white","The chapter distinguishes unsweetened and sweetened condensed milk."],["Why is sweetened condensed milk preserved without heat treatment after packaging?","Its high sugar concentration creates high osmotic pressure","It contains no water","It is always frozen","It contains no microorganisms before processing","High sugar concentration inhibits most microorganisms through osmotic pressure."],["What sugar concentration range in the water phase is stated for sweetened condensed milk?","62.5–64.5%","10–15%","25–30%","90–95%","The chapter specifies 62.5–64.5% sugar in the water phase."],["What is the largest lactose crystal size permitted in first-grade sweetened condensed milk?","10 µm","100 µm","1 mm","1 µm","The handbook states the largest permitted crystal size is 10 µm."],["Why is homogenization used for unsweetened condensed milk?","To disperse fat and prevent fat globules coalescing during sterilization","To create lactose crystals","To add oxygen","To reduce all proteins","Homogenization improves fat dispersion before subsequent sterilization."],["At what temperature range does evaporation of condensed milk typically occur under vacuum?","About 65–70 °C","About 0–5 °C","About 120–140 °C","About 200 °C","The falling-film evaporator boils milk under vacuum at roughly 65–70 °C."],["What is the purpose of pre-heating before evaporation for unsweetened condensed milk?","To destroy microorganisms and improve thermal stability","To freeze the milk","To add lactose","To separate cream only","Pre-heating reduces microbes and stabilizes proteins for later sterilization."],["What temperature range is stated for in-can sterilization of condensed milk?","About 110–120 °C","About 4–8 °C","About 60–70 °C","About 200 °C","The filled cans are heated to roughly 110–120 °C."],["How long is condensed milk held at in-can sterilization temperature?","About 15–20 minutes","About 5 seconds","About 2 hours","About 24 hours","The chapter gives roughly 15–20 minutes at sterilization temperature."],["Why is rapid controlled cooling important for sweetened condensed milk?","To control lactose crystallization and avoid gritty texture","To increase fat separation","To create large sugar crystals","To raise pH","Controlled cooling and seeding produce small lactose crystals and smooth texture."],["Why is preheating condensed-milk feed sometimes integrated with evaporation?","It stabilizes proteins for later severe heat treatment and improves process efficiency","It crystallizes lactose before evaporation","It eliminates the need for homogenization","It lowers dry matter","Preheating denatures some whey proteins and shifts salts to improve heat stability."],["Why must homogenization pressure for unsweetened condensed milk be optimized rather than maximized?","Too much homogenization can reduce protein stability and increase coagulation risk during sterilization","Higher pressure always improves heat stability","Homogenization controls only sweetness","Low pressure always causes gelation","The process balances fat dispersion against heat stability."],["Why are stabilizing salts sometimes added before final sterilization?","They adjust salt balance and improve heat stability of the concentrated milk","They raise sugar concentration for preservation","They remove all calcium","They increase fat globule size","Phosphates are used to improve protein stability during sterilization."],["Why is sample sterilization performed before full-scale processing?","Milk batches differ in heat stability, so the required stabilizer dose must be verified","It measures only can weight","It determines tanker volume","It replaces standardization","Batch variation makes empirical heat-stability testing necessary."],["Why can long cold storage of condensed milk concentrate before sterilization be undesirable?","It can increase the tendency toward age gelation and allow microbial growth","Cold storage always improves heat stability","It removes the need for stabilizer","It converts lactose into sucrose","Extended holding can damage later storage stability."],["Why are cans agitated or kept moving during in-container sterilization?","Movement improves heat distribution through the viscous concentrate","Movement increases sugar crystallization","Movement lowers sterilization temperature to 50 °C","Movement replaces heating","Agitation accelerates and evens heat transfer."],["What causes the light brown color that can develop during severe sterilization?","Maillard reactions between reducing sugars and proteins","Fat crystallization only","Mineral precipitation only","Mechanical homogenization","High heat promotes browning chemistry."],["Why is the sugar concentration in sweetened condensed milk calculated in the water phase?","Microbial inhibition depends on the osmotic pressure of the dissolved sugar in the available water","Only total can weight matters","Sugar acts by increasing fat content","The water phase contains no lactose","Preservation depends on water-phase solute concentration."],["Why are seed lactose crystals added around the supersaturation temperature?","They provide many nuclei so excess lactose forms numerous small crystals instead of a few gritty large ones","They dissolve completely to increase sweetness","They sterilize the product","They reduce milk solids","Seeding controls crystal number and size."],["Why must air entrainment be avoided during rapid cooling and crystallization?","Air can interfere with texture, oxidation and uniform processing","Air ensures smaller lactose crystals","Air increases shelf life","Air is required for osmotic preservation","The process calls for vigorous agitation without entrapping air."]]},"17":{"title":"Milk and Whey Powder","q":[["What are two main reasons for drying food?","Preservation and reduced transport/storage bulk","To increase water activity and weight","To ferment lactose and add air","To raise freezing point and pH","Drying inhibits microorganisms and reduces weight and bulk for transport and storage."],["What does drying primarily remove from dairy products?","Water","Protein","Minerals","All fat","Drying is a mass-transfer process used to remove water."],["For effective hot-air drying, the air should be what?","Hot, dry and moving","Cold, wet and still","Hot and saturated","Cold and motionless","The handbook states effective drying requires hot, dry, moving air."],["Why is whole milk powder's shelf life generally shorter than skim milk powder's?","Fat can oxidize during storage","It contains no protein","It absorbs no water","Skim powder is always frozen","Oxidation of fat can deteriorate whole milk powder flavour."],["What storage environment is recommended for milk powder?","Cool and dry","Hot and humid","Open to air and sunlight","Under running water","Milk powder should be stored in a cool, dry place."],["What is one purpose of agglomerating milk powder?","To improve wettability and reconstitution","To increase moisture content permanently","To reduce particle size to zero","To increase fat oxidation","Agglomeration improves capillarity and therefore wettability."],["What particle-size range is associated with improved wettability in the handbook?","About 130–150 µm","About 1–5 µm","About 1–2 mm","About 5 cm","The handbook notes improved wettability with larger particles around 130–150 µm."],["What is considered a good wettability time?","Less than 30 seconds","More than 30 minutes","Exactly 2 hours","More than 1 day","Good wettability is stated as less than 30 seconds."],["What dispersability is normal for milk powders intended for recombination?","At least about 90%","About 10%","About 30%","Exactly 50%","The text gives at least 90% as a normal dispersability value."],["What solubility index is considered good?","About 0.25 ml undissolved sediment or less in 50 ml","25 ml sediment in 50 ml","50 ml sediment in 50 ml","10 ml sediment in 10 ml","A low solubility index, around 0.25 ml sediment in 50 ml, is considered good."],["Why is milk concentrated before spray drying?","Removing water by evaporation or membranes is far more energy-efficient than removing all of it with hot air","Spray dryers cannot evaporate any water","Concentration increases microbial growth deliberately","Concentration is needed only for color","Pre-concentration reduces the expensive drying load."],["What is the key function of atomization in a spray dryer?","Create many small droplets with high surface area for rapid moisture removal","Separate cream from skim milk","Crystallize lactose before drying","Sterilize air by pressure alone","Drying rate depends strongly on droplet surface area."],["Why can powder become sticky if outlet moisture is too high?","Residual moisture and amorphous components reduce free-flowing behavior and stability","High moisture always improves shelf life","Moisture prevents lactose mobility","Sticky powder indicates too little water","Moisture control is essential for powder handling and storage."],["Why does low-heat skim milk powder have a higher WPNI?","More whey protein remains undenatured","It contains more fat","It has less lactose","It has been sterilized more severely","WPNI measures undenatured whey protein nitrogen."],["Why might high-heat powder be selected despite lower solubility?","Some applications require the functional effects of extensive whey-protein denaturation","High-heat powder always dissolves faster","It contains no protein","It is always used for drinking milk only","Powder heat class is chosen to match end-use functionality."],["Why does agglomeration improve instant powder performance?","Larger porous particles wet, sink and disperse more readily than fine powder","Agglomeration reduces particle size","It removes all air from powder","It increases moisture above safe limits","Agglomerates improve wettability and dispersability."],["Why is air exclusion during whole milk powder production and packaging important?","Milk fat is susceptible to oxidation and off-flavor development","Air increases protein solubility","Oxygen prevents all chemical reactions","Whole milk powder contains no fat","Fat oxidation is a principal storage limitation for whole milk powder."],["Why is whole milk heated around 80–85 °C before drying?","To inactivate lipolytic enzymes that could damage fat during storage","To crystallize all lactose","To remove all protein","To sterilize the powder completely before drying","Lipase inactivation improves fat stability."],["What does a low solubility-index value indicate?","Very little undissolved sediment after reconstitution","Very poor powder dissolution","High bacterial count","High fat oxidation","Lower sediment means better solubility."],["Why can spray-dried powder differ functionally from roller-dried powder?","Drying history and heat exposure affect particle structure, protein denaturation and reconstitution behavior","Both processes are physically identical","Roller drying uses no heat","Spray drying always caramelizes lactose completely","The chosen drying technology influences powder functionality."]]},"18":{"title":"Recombined Milk Products","q":[["What is reconstituted milk?","Milk obtained by adding water to milk powder","Milk made by adding separate milk fat to skim powder and water","Fresh milk with added cream only","Fermented whey","Reconstituted milk is obtained by adding water to SMP, WMP or their mix."],["What distinguishes recombined milk from reconstituted milk?","Milk fat is added separately to skim milk powder and water","It contains no water","It must be fermented","It contains no milk solids","Recombined milk uses SMP plus separately added milk fat and water."],["Which four properties affect milk-powder dissolving quality?","Wettability, ability to sink, dispersability and solubility","Colour, pressure, voltage and pH only","Fat, air, steam and ice","Churning, salting, freezing and ripening","The handbook lists wettability, sinking, dispersability and solubility."],["What water hardness is recommended to be below for recombination?","100 mg/l as CaCO3","1,000 mg/l as CaCO3","500 mg/l as NaCl","No limit is given","Water hardness should be below 100 mg/l expressed as CaCO3."],["At what water temperature range do milk proteins normally hydrate in less than 20 minutes?","40–50 °C","0–5 °C","80–100 °C","120–140 °C","The chapter states normal hydration takes less than 20 minutes at 40–50 °C."],["What is the most common source of milk fat for recombination?","Anhydrous milk fat (AMF)","Only fresh cream","Whey protein isolate","Casein","AMF is described as the most common milk-fat source for recombination."],["What is the recommended maximum copper level in recombination water?","0.05 mg/l","5 mg/l","50 mg/l","500 mg/l","The handbook recommends a maximum of 0.05 mg/l copper."],["What is the recommended maximum iron level in recombination water?","0.1 mg/l","10 mg/l","100 mg/l","1,000 mg/l","The handbook recommends a maximum of 0.1 mg/l iron."],["Why can excessive minerals in recombination water be a problem?","They can upset salt balance and reduce heat stability","They increase powder shelf life","They eliminate the need for pasteurization","They increase lactose sweetness","Water mineral content can affect salt balance and heat stability of the recombined product."],["How long should recombined milk for cheese manufacture be hydrated?","About two hours","About 30 seconds","About 10 minutes","About 24 hours","The chapter recommends about two hours hydration for recombined cheese milk."],["Why is recombination water quality especially critical for UHT products?","Mineral imbalance can reduce heat stability during severe thermal treatment","Hard water always improves protein stability","Copper and iron improve flavor","Water composition is irrelevant once powder dissolves","The handbook warns excess minerals can jeopardize salt balance and heat stability."],["Why should AMF be added above about 40 °C?","It must be fully melted to disperse and emulsify properly","AMF is chemically unstable below 40 °C","Protein hydration stops above 40 °C","Lactose crystallizes at 40 °C","Fat needs to be above its melting point during incorporation."],["Why should recombined milk not remain warm for many hours after mixing?","Warm conditions increase the risk of bacterial growth","Warm holding always improves hydration indefinitely","Fat becomes sterile at high temperature","Protein hydration requires at least 24 hours at 50 °C","The handbook limits warm holding despite improved dissolution."],["Why can insufficient protein hydration create a chalky defect?","Powder particles and proteins have not fully rehydrated into a smooth dispersion","Fat has been over-homogenized","The water is too soft","The product contains too little oxygen","Adequate hydration is needed for smooth product texture."],["Why can mixing at 30 °C create more air in reconstituted skim milk than mixing at 50 °C?","Lower-temperature mixing is associated with higher retained air content","Air solubility and entrainment are unaffected by temperature","Higher temperature always traps more foam","Powder contains no air","The handbook reports significantly higher air content at lower mixing temperature."],["Why is vacuum mixing useful in recombination?","It minimizes foaming and entrained air during powder incorporation","It increases oxidation","It eliminates the need for water","It prevents powder wetting","Continuous air removal reduces foam and downstream problems."],["Why must fat be uniformly dispersed even if a homogenizer follows later?","Non-uniform feed composition causes unstable processing and inconsistent final composition","Homogenizers correct any feed composition instantly regardless of mixing","Fat distribution affects only color","Uniformity is needed only for packaging","Stable feeding and later homogenization require a well-mixed emulsion."],["Why are weighing tanks used for fat dosage in large recombination plants?","They provide the accuracy needed for high-capacity formulation","Weighing tanks are used only for cleaning chemicals","Flow control cannot be used anywhere else","Fat concentration is not important","Large-scale production requires accurate ingredient proportioning."],["Why must mixing tanks have extra headspace?","Foaming during recombination can otherwise force product out of the manhole","Headspace improves UHT lethality","It increases powder solubility chemically","It is needed for freezing","The handbook recommends roughly 20% extra volume because of foaming."],["Why is low-heat versus high-heat SMP selection important in recombined products?","Previous heat treatment changes whey-protein denaturation and therefore functionality and heat stability","All SMP grades behave identically","Heat class controls only bag color","WPNI is unrelated to process behavior","Powder heat history influences reconstitution and final-product properties."]]},"19":{"title":"Ice Cream","q":[["What does 'overrun' mean in ice cream terminology?","Amount of air in the product","Amount of milk fat only","Freezing time","Sugar concentration","The handbook defines overrun as the amount of air in the product."],["What is the typical fat range stated for an ice cream mix?","10–15%","0% always","30–40%","60–70%","The chapter states fat makes up about 10–15% of an ice cream mix."],["What does MSNF include?","Proteins, lactose and mineral salts","Only fat","Only water","Air and flavouring","Milk solids-non-fat consist of proteins, lactose and mineral salts."],["To about what core temperature should ice cream be hardened?","−20 °C","+5 °C","+20 °C","−2 °C","Manufacture is not complete until ice cream is hardened to a core temperature around −20 °C."],["At approximately what temperature is hardened ice cream stored?","−25 °C","+4 °C","+15 °C","0 °C","After hardening, the handbook gives cold storage at about −25 °C."],["What is one function of fat in ice cream?","It contributes creaminess and melting resistance","It removes all air","It prevents freezing","It eliminates sweetness","Fat helps creaminess and stabilizes the air-cell structure."],["What ratio of MSNF to water is described as optimal in a balanced recipe?","17 parts MSNF to 100 parts water","1 part to 1 part","50 parts to 10 parts","100 parts to 1 part","The handbook gives 17 parts MSNF per 100 parts water as an optimal level."],["What storage temperature is stated for milk products used in ice-cream manufacture?","Below 5 °C","Above 30 °C","Exactly 20 °C","Above 60 °C","Liquid milk products are stored below 5 °C."],["At what temperature is AMF or melted butter typically held for ice-cream use?","About 35–40 °C","About 0 °C","About 90 °C","About −25 °C","Melted milk fat is held at around 35–40 °C for pumping."],["What are the three product-forming categories described?","Moulded, filled and extruded","Pasteurized, sterilized and dried","Sweet, sour and neutral","Batch, tank and pipe","The chapter groups products by moulded, filled and extruded processing."],["Why is ageing of ice-cream mix performed before freezing?","It allows fat crystallization and stabilizer hydration, improving whipping and texture","It raises the mix temperature for bacterial growth","It removes all sugar","It sterilizes the mix","Ageing develops the physical state needed for freezing and air incorporation."],["Why is overrun a critical process parameter rather than just a volume measurement?","It affects texture, body, melting behavior and product yield","It measures only sugar concentration","It determines pasteurization temperature","It is unrelated to sensory quality","Air content changes both economics and product structure."],["Why must air cells be small and well distributed?","Fine, uniform cells give smoother texture and better structural stability","Large cells always improve creaminess","Air-cell size affects only color","Air is removed during hardening","The dispersed air phase is a major part of ice-cream structure."],["Why does fast hardening improve texture?","Rapid freezing limits growth of large ice crystals","Slow freezing creates smaller crystals","Hardening only affects packaging","Ice crystals do not change after the freezer","Fast heat removal preserves a fine crystal structure."],["Why is the product not considered fully manufactured immediately after leaving the continuous freezer?","It still requires hardening to a much lower core temperature for structural stability","It contains no air yet","It has not been pasteurized","It has no sugar","The freezer partially freezes the mix; hardening completes freezing."],["Why are stabilizers used in ice cream?","They help control water mobility, ice crystal growth and melting properties","They replace milk fat completely","They sterilize the product","They add overrun directly","Stabilizers support physical stability during freezing and storage."],["Why are emulsifiers useful even though milk already contains natural emulsifiers?","They modify fat destabilization and help build the air/fat structure during freezing","They dissolve all ice crystals","They prevent any fat interaction","They are added only for sweetness","Controlled fat destabilization contributes to body and meltdown."],["Why must storage temperature remain consistently low after hardening?","Temperature fluctuations promote recrystallization and texture deterioration","Warm cycles improve texture","Storage temperature affects only package color","Ice cream is stable at any temperature below room temperature","Stable low temperature minimizes crystal growth."],["Why does MSNF have an upper practical limit in formulation?","Too much lactose and salts can create sandy texture or other balance problems","MSNF contains no lactose","More MSNF always improves every property","MSNF affects only color","Recipe balance considers solids concentration and lactose behavior."],["Why are fat and emulsifier levels linked to extrusion performance?","Product firmness and structural stability must support shape retention after the freezer","Extrusion uses only water","Fat has no effect on mechanical properties","Emulsifier prevents freezing","Extruded novelties need sufficient body to hold shape during hardening."]]},"20":{"title":"Casein","q":[["About what percentage of cow's milk protein is casein?","80%","20%","5%","100%","Casein comprises about 80% of the total protein in cow's milk."],["What are the two main commercial casein production methods?","Acid precipitation and rennet coagulation","Freezing and evaporation","Homogenization and aeration","Churning and salting","Commercial casein is mainly made by acid precipitation or rennet coagulation."],["At what approximate pH is acid casein precipitated?","4.6–4.7","6.7–7.0","8.0–9.0","2.0","Acid casein is obtained by acidifying skim milk to its isoelectric point around pH 4.6–4.7."],["Why is low fat important in skim milk for casein production?","Fat is detrimental to casein quality","Fat is required for acidification","Fat prevents drying","Fat is needed for rennet activity","Small amounts of fat are detrimental to casein quality, so efficient separation is important."],["Why is casein washed after whey drainage?","To remove whey proteins, lactose and salts","To add lactose","To increase fat","To introduce air","Washing removes residual whey proteins, lactose and salts."],["What temperature is used for rennet casein coagulation after cooling?","About 30 °C","About 4 °C","About 80 °C","About 120 °C","For rennet casein, milk is cooled to about 30 °C before rennet is added."],["How long does the rennet-casein gel take to form?","About 15–20 minutes","About 5 seconds","About 5 hours","About 24 hours","The chapter states a gel forms after roughly 15–20 minutes."],["Why is the curd heated to about 60 °C in rennet casein production?","To deactivate the enzyme","To freeze the curd","To add fat","To increase lactose","Heating to around 60 °C deactivates the rennet enzyme."],["How many washing stages are typically used for rennet casein?","Two or three","None","Ten to twelve","Only one always","The curd is washed in two or three stages."],["Why is countercurrent washing preferred?","It uses less water","It increases lactose retention","It raises fat content","It prevents drying","Countercurrent washing reduces water consumption substantially compared with concurrent washing."],["Why must skim milk for casein production have very low fat content?","Residual fat reduces chemical purity and product quality","Fat is needed for acid precipitation","Fat improves washing efficiency","Fat is converted to casein during drying","Casein quality standards require efficient separation of milk fat."],["Why is multistage washing essential after casein precipitation?","It removes whey proteins, lactose and salts that reduce purity and keeping quality","It adds minerals to the curd","It increases fat content","It polymerizes casein","Commercial casein requires removal of non-casein milk solids."],["Why is countercurrent washing more water-efficient?","Freshest water contacts the cleanest curd and wash water is reused progressively upstream","All stages use fresh water simultaneously","Countercurrent flow increases lactose retention","It eliminates dewatering","Countercurrent contact maximizes washing driving force with less water."],["Why is mineral-acid casein heated after acidification?","Heating promotes aggregation and syneresis so curd can be separated efficiently","Heating raises pH back to neutral","Heating converts casein to whey protein","Heating adds calcium","Temperature assists curd formation and whey release."],["Why does acidification near pH 4.6 precipitate casein?","Casein micelles approach their isoelectric condition and lose electrostatic stability","Lactose becomes insoluble at pH 4.6","Milk fat becomes negatively charged enough to precipitate","All minerals disappear","Charge neutralization promotes casein aggregation."],["Why is rennet casein heated to around 60 °C after coagulation?","To deactivate chymosin and firm the curd for subsequent processing","To reactivate the enzyme","To dissolve the curd","To increase milk fat","Cooking controls enzyme activity and curd properties."],["Why can excessive lactose carryover darken dried rennet casein?","Residual lactose can participate in heat-related browning reactions","Lactose is naturally black","Lactose lowers fat content","Lactose prevents drying","Dark color is associated with inferior purity and residual lactose."],["Why can sodium caseinate solutions become difficult to spray dry at high solids?","Their viscosity becomes very high even at moderate concentration","Caseinate contains no protein","High solids reduce viscosity to water-like levels","Spray drying requires crystallized lactose only","Viscosity limits solids content before atomization."],["Why is casein curd milled before addition of alkali in sodium caseinate manufacture?","Smaller particles dissolve and react with alkali more rapidly","Milling raises pH directly","Milling sterilizes the curd","Larger particles react faster","Particle-size reduction speeds dissolution and neutralization."],["What distinguishes co-precipitate from ordinary acid casein?","Co-precipitate retains whey proteins along with casein","It contains no milk protein","It is produced only from butter","It contains only lactose","Heating and controlled precipitation capture a casein/whey-protein complex."]]},"21":{"title":"Cleaning of Dairy Equipment","q":[["What does CIP mean?","Cleaning-in-place","Cooling-in-process","Control-in-pasteurization","Cleaning-in-packaging only","CIP means cleaning-in-place: circulating cleaning liquids without dismantling equipment."],["What creates the mechanical scouring effect in a CIP circuit?","High-velocity liquid flow","Static storage","Low temperature alone","Air exposure","High-velocity flow over equipment surfaces helps dislodge deposits."],["Why must detergent be circulated for sufficient time?","Deposits need time to dissolve","To increase milk fat","To create foam for packaging","To cool the plant","Cleaning time must be long enough for the detergent to dissolve deposits."],["Why must equipment be rinsed thoroughly after detergent cleaning?","Residual detergent can contaminate milk","To increase detergent concentration","To add minerals","To promote bacterial growth","All detergent traces must be removed to prevent contamination."],["What two forms of cleaning verification are stated?","Visual and bacteriological inspection","Taste and colour only","Pressure and flow only","Weight and volume only","The handbook identifies visual and bacteriological inspection as verification methods."],["What flow velocity range is given for CIP detergent circulation?","About 1.5–3.0 m/s","About 0.01–0.02 m/s","About 10–20 m/s","No flow is used","Detergent circulation is designed for turbulent flow around 1.5–3.0 m/s."],["Why are both alkaline and acid detergents used on heavily fouled heat exchangers?","Different deposits require different chemical actions","To increase milk fat","To reduce pipe diameter","To add flavour","Protein and mineral deposits require complementary alkaline and acid cleaning."],["How long may heavily encrusted heat exchangers require combined alkaline/acid circulation?","About 20–60 minutes","About 5 seconds","About 2 minutes","About 24 hours","The chapter gives a total cleaning exposure of roughly 20–60 minutes."],["What water is preferred for final rinsing?","Softened water","Seawater","Untreated hard water","Milk","Softened water helps prevent lime-scale deposits."],["What pH below is suggested for acidified final rinse water to inhibit bacterial growth?","Below pH 5","Above pH 10","Exactly pH 7","Below pH 1 only","Acidifying the final rinse to below pH 5 helps prevent overnight bacterial growth."],["Why is cleaning effectiveness determined by a combination of time, temperature, concentration and mechanical action?","Weakness in one factor may require compensation by the others to remove soil effectively","Only detergent concentration matters","Temperature alone guarantees cleanliness","Flow velocity has no effect","CIP performance depends on several interacting cleaning factors."],["Why must CIP flow be turbulent rather than merely filling the pipe?","High wall shear helps remove deposits from product-contact surfaces","Turbulence reduces detergent contact","Laminar flow always cleans faster","Flow regime affects only pump noise","Mechanical scouring is a major cleaning mechanism."],["Why are alkaline detergents especially useful against protein and fat soils?","Alkali helps dissolve proteins and saponify/emulsify fats","Alkali removes only mineral scale","Alkali is used only for final rinse","Alkali has no chemical cleaning action","Different soil classes require appropriate detergent chemistry."],["Why is an acid step often needed after alkaline cleaning of heated equipment?","Acid removes mineral deposits such as milkstone that alkali does not remove effectively","Acid is used to add protein","Acid neutralizes all cleaning action before it starts","Acid replaces the water rinse","Heat surfaces accumulate mineral scale requiring acid cleaning."],["Why is final rinsing continued until detergent residues are removed?","Chemical residues can contaminate product and interfere with processing","Residual detergent improves product flavor","Detergent is required in finished milk","Rinsing is only for cooling equipment","CIP must leave surfaces chemically clean as well as visually clean."],["Why can decentralized CIP reduce water and steam use?","Only the required small circuit volume is prepared and circulated at high flow","It uses larger tanks for every circuit","It keeps all circuits full continuously","It eliminates detergent","Small-batch circuit-specific cleaning reduces total liquid inventory."],["Why might single-use detergent be acceptable in decentralized CIP?","The solution can be optimized for a specific circuit and discarded after its soil load","Single-use detergent is always cheaper regardless of system","Recycled detergent cannot clean dairy equipment","Detergent concentration never changes during use","Circuit-specific chemistry can make reuse less necessary."],["Why is visual inspection alone insufficient in highly automated closed plants?","Many internal surfaces are inaccessible, so bacteriological monitoring is needed","Automated plants never require verification","Visual inspection measures microbial counts directly","CIP automatically guarantees sterility","Closed systems require indirect hygiene verification at strategic points."],["Why are coliform swab tests useful after cleaning?","They provide an indicator of hygienic cleaning performance on product-contact surfaces","They measure fat residue only","They determine detergent concentration","They replace all product testing","Coliform monitoring is one of the handbook's stated CIP verification methods."],["Why should cleaning programs be designed around the specific equipment and soil rather than one universal recipe?","Heat exchangers, tanks and pipelines accumulate different deposits and have different hydraulic conditions","All dairy soils are chemically identical","Equipment geometry does not affect cleaning","CIP is independent of product history","Effective CIP matches chemistry and circulation conditions to the actual cleaning task."]]},"22":{"title":"Dairy Effluent","q":[["What type of pollutants are particularly significant from the food industry?","Organic pollutants","Only radioactive pollutants","Only metals","Only gases","The chapter emphasizes organic pollution from food processing."],["Organic pollutants are described approximately as what distribution?","One-third dissolved, one-third colloidal, one-third suspended","All dissolved","All suspended","Half gas and half solid","The handbook describes organic pollutants as roughly equal thirds dissolved, colloidal and suspended."],["What should be done with curd spilled on the floor?","Sweep it up and treat it as solid waste","Flush it into the drain","Return it directly to the cheese vat","Dissolve it with acid","The chapter recommends treating spilled curd as solid waste rather than flushing it away."],["What should be done with whey where possible?","Find commercial uses instead of discharging it as waste","Always discharge it to sewer","Mix it with cleaning chemicals","Evaporate it into the room","The handbook encourages recovery/commercial use of whey rather than disposal."],["What does a venturi flume system in the chapter measure?","Waste-water flow in an open canal","Milk fat content","Ice cream overrun","Casein particle size","Figure 22.1 illustrates measuring waste-water flow in an open canal with a venturi flume."],["What does BOD relate to in effluent treatment?","The organic pollution load requiring biological oxygen","Milk fat percentage","Electrical power demand","Ice-cream overrun","BOD is used as an indicator of biodegradable organic pollution load."],["What is the first stage in traditional sewage treatment?","Mechanical removal/sedimentation of solids","UHT treatment","Homogenization","Spray drying","Mechanical treatment removes the bulk of solid impurities."],["What does biological treatment mainly do?","Decompose organic compounds","Increase milk fat","Remove all minerals by centrifugation","Add oxygen to packaged milk","The biological stage reduces biodegradable organic material."],["Why is chemical treatment added in some plants?","To reduce pollutants such as phosphorus","To pasteurize wastewater","To produce yoghurt culture","To increase lactose","Chemical precipitation can be used to reduce phosphorus."],["What reduction is cited for phosphorus with pre-precipitation?","About 90%","About 5%","About 25%","100% in all cases","The chapter states pre-precipitation can reduce phosphorus by about 90%."],["Why is dairy wastewater often characterized by high organic load?","Lost milk, whey, fat and product residues contain biodegradable organic matter","Cleaning water contains no organics","Only minerals contribute to dairy pollution","Dairy effluent is mainly atmospheric water","Product losses are a major source of BOD/COD."],["Why is prevention of product loss usually better than treating it in wastewater later?","Recovering product avoids both economic loss and effluent-treatment load","Treatment always creates more saleable product","Wastewater plants require product to function","Product recovery increases BOD","Source reduction is both economical and environmentally effective."],["Why should curd be swept up rather than hosed into the drain?","Solid recovery prevents a concentrated organic load from entering the wastewater system","Water dissolves curd harmlessly","Curd has no BOD","Hosing improves treatment efficiency","Keeping solids out of drains reduces pollutant load."],["Why is whey recovery particularly important from an effluent perspective?","Whey has high organic value and pollution load, so commercial use avoids major BOD discharge","Whey contains only water","Whey cannot be processed into products","Whey reduces BOD when discharged","The handbook encourages commercial use rather than disposal."],["What is the purpose of flow-proportional wastewater sampling?","Create a representative composite sample that reflects changes in discharge volume","Take only the dirtiest sample","Measure milk fat continuously","Replace flow measurement","Aliquot sampling proportional to flow gives a representative daily sample."],["Why is mechanical treatment normally placed before biological treatment?","It removes suspended solids and reduces the load on the biological stage","Biological treatment creates the solids first","Mechanical treatment sterilizes water completely","Order has no process effect","Primary removal simplifies downstream treatment."],["Why does pre-precipitation reduce the size and energy demand of the biological stage?","Much of the phosphorus and BOD is removed earlier, so biology receives a lighter load","It increases BOD before biology","It eliminates all water","It adds more organic material","The handbook notes substantial pollutant removal before biological treatment."],["Why can phosphorus require a chemical treatment step?","Biological and mechanical stages alone may not achieve required phosphorus removal","Phosphorus is removed only by evaporation","Phosphorus is a gas","Chemical precipitation increases phosphorus concentration intentionally","Chemical precipitation is used to meet tighter phosphorus limits."],["Why is outlet flow continuously measured in many regulated systems?","Pollutant discharge must be quantified as both concentration and total load","Only temperature matters legally","Flow is unrelated to effluent control","Measurement is used only for pump sizing","Compliance and sampling depend on knowing discharge volume."],["Why should local authorities be consulted early when planning effluent treatment?","Required discharge limits determine the treatment train and design basis","Authorities select milk recipes","Effluent plants have no legal constraints","Treatment design is identical everywhere","Permitted BOD, phosphorus and discharge conditions vary by location."]]}};